window.DOCS_INDEX = [{"slug":"index","title":"Overview","group":"Start here","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"running-it","text":"Running it"},{"level":2,"id":"controls","text":"Controls"},{"level":2,"id":"url-parameters","text":"URL parameters"},{"level":2,"id":"deploying","text":"Deploying"},{"level":2,"id":"privacy-and-licenses","text":"Privacy and licenses"},{"level":2,"id":"pages","text":"Pages"}],"text":"Untrue: documentation How the game works, from world generation to the living world. Untrue is an endless 2D procedural survival and exploration game for the browser. The world is generated around the player in chunks and thrown away behind them, so places can come back different on a later visit. It looks and feels like Stardew Valley: top-down pixel art with a warm, cozy tone. The player is an adventurer who explores, gathers, crafts and fights, but never builds. Every structure is forgotten once the player leaves it far behind. Since rifts tore open all over it, the world has been forgetting itself; stepping through one leads into an outer dimension of challenges (story.md, dimensions.md). Running it Command What it does npm install Installs dependencies (PixiJS, Vite, TypeScript, Vitest). npm run dev Starts the Vite dev server. npm test Runs the Vitest suites once. npm run build Type-checks with tsc --noEmit, then builds to dist/. npm run preview Serves the production build. npm run docs Builds this documentation as a website in docs-site/; open docs-site/index.html. Controls Input Action WASD or arrow keys Move Shift Run (uses stamina) Left click or J Attack (toward the mouse, or the way you face) Right click (hold) or K (hold) Block Space Dodge roll (not while swimming); advances a story vision E Talk, read, gather, open, drink, rest, enter a building or a rift, take the stairs, open a dimension's vault; advances a story vision 1 to 8 Select a hotbar slot F Use the selected item Q Drop the selected hotbar stack Tab or I Open or close the menu M Open or close the map, where maps work: only inside an outer dimension (the overworld won't hold still) Escape Close a dialog or the menu; otherwise pause Ctrl+S Save now Mouse wheel, + and − Zoom F3 Debug overlay G, then B Debug, development builds only: jump 20,000 tiles east, then come back Changing keys. On desktop, Settings → Keys lists each action with its main key. Click one, then press the new key (Esc cancels). A key does one thing: if another action had it, that action gets the old key back. \"Reset keys\" restores the defaults. The arrow keys, right Shift, I (bag) and the numpad + and − always work too. Esc, F3, F4 and 1–8 are kept for the game and cannot be bound. Keys are physical positions (KeyboardEvent.code), so WASD sits in the same place on any layout. The controls bar, the pause menu and the tips name the player's own keys. Code: src/game/Keys.ts. Touch screens get: a virtual stick (drag on the left half of the game; a faint one rests bottom left), tap-to-interact (a quick tap anywhere on the game, or on the action prompt, acts like E), pinch to zoom: two fingers together zoom instead of steering (the second finger counts as a pinch if it lands within 250 ms of the first, or if the first is not steering), Use, Bag, Hit, Roll and hold-to-Block buttons; Hit aims at the nearest enemy in reach, small Pause and Full buttons above the action buttons. Full is full screen (also in the pause menu). iPhones have no full screen for pages, so the button is hidden there; Add to Home Screen opens the game full screen instead (public/manifest.webmanifest). Tapping a hotbar slot selects it and shows the item's name (and a weapon's durability) above the bar for a moment; tapping it again uses it. On touch screens the vitals sit under the compass and the buttons bottom right, and the pause menu lists the touch controls. URL parameters Parameter Effect ?new Start a new world with a random seed, ignoring the save. ?seed=N Start a new world with seed N, ignoring the save. ?spawn=x,y Spawn at tile x,y (development builds only). ?hour=H Set the clock to hour H (development builds only). ?weather=kind Force a weather kind in every biome for 24 hours (development builds only): clear, rain, storm, fog, sandstorm, heat, snow, blizzard. ?vitals=N Start health, stamina, hunger and thirst at N% (development builds only), to see the low warnings. ?playground or ?playground=SEED Story playground (development builds only): a fresh world, never saved, with a panel (F4 or tapping its title folds it) to travel to the spawn, set night or morning, toggle god mode and restart, and for the rifts: tear one open nearby, enter The Hollow, and skip to its combat, path, puzzle, boss or vault chamber. ?fps=30 Cap the frame rate at 30 (it is otherwise capped at 60). The game strips the query string after reading it, so reloading continues the same world. In dev builds (npm run dev), the running game is available in the browser console as window.game. Deploying npm run build writes a static site to dist/: upload the folder to any static host (Netlify, GitHub Pages, itch.io, an S3 bucket, nginx). Paths are relative, so it works from a subfolder too. The game code (about 100 KB gzipped) and Pixi (about 160 KB gzipped) are separate files, so players keep Pixi cached across updates; serve dist/assets/ with long cache headers, since its file names change with every build. Pixi's WebGPU code ships inside the Pixi file even though the game uses WebGL: Pixi's main export imports it statically, so splitting it out would need a custom Pixi build, and vite.config.ts does not try. On Vercel: import the project (or run vercel in its folder). vercel.json already sets everything: npm ci, npm run build, output dist/, a year of immutable caching for dist/assets/, and a few safe security headers. Node 22.12 or newer is required (engines in package.json; Vercel's default Node 22 is fine). No environment variables or server functions are needed: the game is entirely static, and saves stay in each player's browser. The game makes no outside requests: the Pixelify Sans font ships with it (@fontsource/pixelify-sans, imported at the top of src/style.css so the opening screen has it from the first paint), so no player data reaches Google Fonts. What keeps a live build safe: Saves live in IndexedDB in the player's browser. If storage is blocked (some private modes), the game still runs and says once that it cannot save. A save that cannot be read (for example, one from a newer version) is kept as a backup and a new world begins, with an alert, instead of the game failing to start. If storage fails to read at all, that session does not save, so it cannot overwrite a save it never saw. Older save versions are upgraded. See saves.md. World generation runs in web workers, and falls back to the main thread if workers cannot start or one fails, so the world always loads. Errors in a frame are logged and the game carries on (Pixi would otherwise stop ticking). If the game cannot start at all (no WebGL), the page says so. Cheats are off: ?spawn, ?hour, ?weather, the G/B teleport and window.game only exist in development builds. ?seed, ?new and ?fps=30 work everywhere. Two tabs: only the first tab saves (a Web Lock, with a BroadcastChannel fallback); a later tab plays without saving and says so. Touch screens get a left-half stick, tap-to-interact, pinch zoom, a tappable action prompt, Use, Bag, Hit, Roll, hold-to-Block, Pause and Full buttons, and a layout that fits phones upright or sideways. Accessibility: screen flashes can be turned off (and are off by default under the system's reduced-motion setting, which also stops HUD animation), the text and HUD size runs from 0.8 to 1.5, desktop keys can be changed, and first-time tips explain each part of the game as it comes up. Privacy and licenses No personal data: no accounts, analytics, ads or cookies. Saves (IndexedDB) and settings (localStorage) stay in the player's browser, which counts as strictly necessary storage, so no consent banner is needed. The host's server logs (Vercel) are the only data anyone else sees. About tab (beside Settings in the menu, src/ui/About.ts): a short privacy note, credits (original art and sound) and the open source licenses, with the full texts on demand. Third-party notices: npm run build first runs scripts/build-notices.mjs (also npm run notices), which writes the license of every shipped package (Pixi.js and its dependencies, Pixelify Sans under the SIL OFL 1.1) to public/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt. Type-only packages are skipped. Before the app stores, analytics, ads, accounts or sales: the stores want a privacy policy URL; tracking needs a full privacy policy and consent; selling needs a trademark check on the name. Pages Engine: Architecture: source layout, the frame loop, threads, how systems talk, testing. World streaming: chunks, the render, cache and simulation areas, the chunk lifecycle. World generation: noise fields, tiles, decorations, biomes, regions and regeneration. Objects and structures: world objects, layouts, the structure catalogue, placement, and how every structure is forgotten. Saves: what is saved, the save format and its versions. Rendering: the procedural atlas, meshes, sprites, lighting, weather, effects and UI. Gameplay: Survival Combat Living world Story: the rifts, and the objective in the tracker Dimensions: The Hollow behind the rifts, its challenges, the map, and dying there Buildings and interiors: building sprites, walls with height, and walking inside Exploration: the map (not in the overworld), fog of war, markers and the compass Audio: synthesised sound effects, music and weather ambience Design: Design spec See also: Architecture "},{"slug":"design-spec","title":"Design spec","group":"Start here","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"1-overview","text":"1. Overview"},{"level":3,"id":"design-pillars","text":"Design pillars"},{"level":2,"id":"2-world-streaming","text":"2. World streaming"},{"level":3,"id":"21-radii","text":"2.1 Radii"},{"level":3,"id":"22-render-margin","text":"2.2 Render margin"},{"level":3,"id":"23-chunk-lifecycle","text":"2.3 Chunk lifecycle"},{"level":2,"id":"3-procedural-generation","text":"3. Procedural generation"},{"level":3,"id":"31-terrain","text":"3.1 Terrain"},{"level":3,"id":"32-biomes","text":"3.2 Biomes"},{"level":3,"id":"33-structures-and-points-of-interest","text":"3.3 Structures and points of interest"},{"level":3,"id":"34-randomness","text":"3.4 Randomness"},{"level":3,"id":"35-world-scale-rules","text":"3.5 World-scale rules"},{"level":3,"id":"36-rare-events","text":"3.6 Rare events"},{"level":2,"id":"4-object-lifetime-and-persistence","text":"4. Object lifetime and persistence"},{"level":3,"id":"41-multi-chunk-objects","text":"4.1 Multi-chunk objects"},{"level":3,"id":"42-temporary-world-state","text":"4.2 Temporary world state"},{"level":3,"id":"43-persistence-tiers","text":"4.3 Persistence tiers"},{"level":2,"id":"5-gameplay-systems","text":"5. Gameplay systems"},{"level":2,"id":"6-living-world","text":"6. Living world"},{"level":3,"id":"61-time-and-weather","text":"6.1 Time and weather"},{"level":3,"id":"62-simulation","text":"6.2 Simulation"},{"level":3,"id":"63-quests-and-world-events","text":"6.3 Quests and world events"},{"level":3,"id":"64-exploration","text":"6.4 Exploration"},{"level":2,"id":"7-presentation","text":"7. Presentation"},{"level":3,"id":"71-visual-direction","text":"7.1 Visual direction"},{"level":3,"id":"72-hud-layout","text":"7.2 HUD layout"},{"level":3,"id":"73-features","text":"7.3 Features"},{"level":2,"id":"8-technical-design","text":"8. Technical design"},{"level":3,"id":"81-tech-stack","text":"8.1 Tech stack"},{"level":3,"id":"82-threading","text":"8.2 Threading"},{"level":3,"id":"83-performance-techniques","text":"8.3 Performance techniques"},{"level":3,"id":"84-save-system","text":"8.4 Save system"},{"level":3,"id":"85-project-layout","text":"8.5 Project layout"},{"level":2,"id":"9-roadmap","text":"9. Roadmap"},{"level":3,"id":"91-mvp-build-order","text":"9.1 MVP build order"},{"level":3,"id":"92-after-the-mvp","text":"9.2 After the MVP"},{"level":2,"id":"10-open-questions","text":"10. Open questions"}],"text":"Endless 2D Procedural Survival Game: Design Spec 1. Overview An endless 2D survival and exploration game whose world is generated on demand and discarded once the player leaves. Because discarded areas are not stored, they can regenerate differently on a later visit. The world is memory-efficient and evolving rather than infinitely saved. Its look, UI and feel follow Stardew Valley: top-down pixel art with a warm, cozy tone. The game's story, The World That Forgets, lives in its own document: docs/story.md. It turns the world's regeneration into the story's central mystery. Design pillars Endless world. No map boundary. Only chunks near the player exist in memory. Fully procedural. Terrain, biomes, structures, resources and enemies come from rules and seeded randomness, not stored maps. Multi-chunk objects. A castle, lake or mountain can span many chunks and still behave as one object. Temporary world state. An object lives while any chunk it occupies is active, then is discarded. Evolving world. Discarded areas can regenerate differently. Only objects marked persistent are guaranteed to remain. The player is an adventurer. They explore, fight and gather, but never build houses or place building blocks. 2. World streaming The world is a grid of fixed-size chunks with coordinates that extend indefinitely. Chunks are generated around the player and disposed of behind them. 2.1 Radii The render area is derived from the screen. The simulation and cache radii are configurable. Current values: chunks are 32 × 32 tiles of 16 px, the simulation radius is 2 chunks around the player's chunk, and the cache radius is the render area plus 2 chunks on every side. Radius What happens inside it Render Chunks are drawn Simulation Entities, AI and world events are updated Cache Chunks stay in memory but are not simulated 2.2 Render margin The render area is always the visible area plus a one-chunk margin on every side. A screen that shows 4 × 4 chunks renders 6 × 6. The render area scales the same way with screen size and zoom, so the player never sees a chunk appear at the screen edge. The cache radius must always cover the render area. 2.3 Chunk lifecycle Predict. Queue chunks ahead of the player, based on direction of travel. Generate. Build queued chunks in the background, nearest and most likely first. Activate. Start rendering and simulating a chunk when it enters the relevant radius. Deactivate. Stop simulating a chunk when it leaves the simulation radius. Dispose. Destroy a chunk when it leaves the cache radius and return it to the pool. 3. Procedural generation Everything in the world is computed from rules and a seed. Nothing is authored as a fixed map. 3.1 Terrain Terrain is shaped by layered noise fields. The same fields pick a biome's type when the biome is first generated (section 3.2): Height + Moisture + Temperature + Erosion + Randomness → Biome Category Features Landforms Mountains, hills, plains, valleys, cliffs, beaches Water Rivers, lakes, marshes (a forest landscape zone of shallow pools, beyond 200 tiles from the start) Underground Mines (caves) and keep cellars, entered through buildings. There is no separate underground layer. Climate regions Forests, deserts, snow regions 3.2 Biomes Each biome defines its own terrain, resources, enemies, structures and weather. Biomes blend at their borders and can have rare variants. Biome Features Enemies Structures Forest Trees, mushrooms, marshes, wildlife Wolves, bears, spiders, thorn spiders Ruins, cabins, villages, castles Desert Cactus, oases, dune seas, sandstorms Scorpions, emperor scorpions, sand lurkers, jackals Ruins, nomad camps, the Sun Temple Snow Snow, ice, taiga, blizzards Frost golems, frost wolves, tundra bears, wolves Cabins, villages, castles Wraiths roam every biome at night. Biome size and lifetime A biome is generated once, as a single region, the first time any part of it enters the cache area. Its size and shape then stay fixed for as long as it is loaded, so every chunk inside it generates consistently. Trigger. A chunk enters the cache radius where no biome record exists. (Cache rather than render, so structures anchored next to the render area know their biome before they are drawn.) Type. The biome type is picked from the climate noise at that point and the world-scale rules. Growth prefers chunks whose climate matches, so an area keeps its type across regenerations. Size. A size is rolled, never below the minimum biome size. Shape. The region grows outward from that point with noise-roughened borders. It stops where it meets a neighbouring biome that is already loaded. Store. The biome record (type, footprint, borders) is kept in memory. Generate. Every chunk in the footprint reads the record for its terrain, resources, enemies and structures. Discard. The record is dropped once the player has left the biome and none of its chunks remain inside the cache radius. Rules: Minimum size. Every biome covers at least a fixed minimum area, set by one hardcoded constant (64 chunks). This prevents slivers and tiny patches. A biome hemmed in below the minimum joins the neighbour it touches most. One record, many chunks. A biome follows the same lifetime rule as a multi-chunk object (section 4.1). Temporary by default. A discarded biome is not saved. The same area can generate with a different size, shape, variant and contents on a later visit. Its type stays fixed by climate. 3.3 Structures and points of interest Structures are placed by probability plus rules. Larger ones can span multiple chunks (see section 4). Rarity Examples Common Small houses, camps, trees, rocks, resource deposits Uncommon Ruins, towers, cabins, abandoned camps, mines, nomad camps (desert only) Rare Villages, temples, castles, dungeons, ancient ruins Extremely rare Giant cities, special temples, boss arenas, legendary locations, unique landmarks Each structure definition carries these placement inputs: Probability Biome requirement Terrain requirement Distance requirement Size Orientation 3.4 Randomness Seeded RNG with independent random streams per system Weighted selection from probability tables Rarity tiers and spawn probabilities Minimum-distance and maximum-density rules Regional variation Random rotation and variants for structures Example loot table for a chest: Rarity Chance Common 60% Uncommon 25% Rare 10% Epic 4% Legendary 1% 3.5 World-scale rules Global patterns sit on top of local generation. Difficulty is hardcoded. The game has no difficulty setting for the player to choose. It rises with distance from the origin in fixed bands: Distance from origin (tiles) Difficulty 0 to 1,000 Easy 1,000 to 5,000 Medium 5,000 to 10,000 Hard 10,000+ Extreme Other world-scale rules: Difficulty bands (the table above) scale enemy health, damage and XP, elite chances, chest rolls, quest rewards and the odds of legendary creatures. Rare structures appear farther from the origin: each has a minimum distance, and rarer tiers weigh more in higher bands. Rarer finds further out: the chance of gold ore from ore (0.12 + 0.05 × band, with an iron pickaxe), of a gem from a crystal (0.06 + 0.03 × band) and of an extra crystal (0.15 × band) grows per band. Landscape zones vary each biome: meadows and deep woods, marshes, dune seas, open snowfields and taiga, rock fields, and oases. Safe zone: the start is always forest, and no wild enemy spawns within 64 tiles of the origin. Dangerous zones: the far bands are grimmer, with a faint gloom over the light, lower and darker music, and more legendary creatures. Biomes and resources are distributed by region. 3.6 Rare events Very low-probability finds give each player discoveries that others may never see. Find Chance Ancient temple 0.1% Legendary creature 0.05% × (1 + band) Lost city 0.01% Extremely rare landmark 0.001% Rare events roll per chunk. The legendary creature rolls once per chunk per region visit. Each land has its own legend: the Elder Stag near home, the Alpha Wolf further out (band 2 and beyond, or the snow from band 1), the Dune Tyrant in the desert (band 1 and beyond) and the Ancient Golem in the snow (band 1 and beyond). 4. Object lifetime and persistence This is the system that defines the game. A world object is discarded only when every chunk it occupies has left the active range. In this section, active means loaded: the chunk is still inside the cache radius. 4.1 Multi-chunk objects Each object records the chunks it occupies. The object stays alive while any of those chunks is active. It is discarded only when all of them are inactive. As built, multi-chunk objects are structures (buildings, villages, castles, dungeons) and lakes. Forests, rivers and mountains are terrain from seeded noise, not objects, so they need no lifetime of their own. A giant castle might cover a 3 × 3 block of chunks: ┌─────────┬─────────┬─────────┐ │ Chunk 1 │ Chunk 2 │ Chunk 3 │ ├─────────┼─────────┼─────────┤ │ Chunk 4 │ Chunk 5 │ Chunk 6 │ ├─────────┼─────────┼─────────┤ │ Chunk 7 │ Chunk 8 │ Chunk 9 │ └─────────┴─────────┴─────────┘ 4.2 Temporary world state The player approaches and the area is generated. Objects exist in memory. The player moves away. Objects remain while partially active. An object's whole footprint leaves the active area. The object is discarded. The area can regenerate differently on a later visit. Example: the first visit finds a forest with a cabin. A later visit can find the forest with ruins instead, or a different biome. 4.3 Persistence tiers Tier Behaviour Temporary Discarded and free to regenerate differently. The default for everything. Semi-persistent Between the two: once discovered, kept for 3 in-game days after the player leaves (villages, castles, mines, nomad camps). Persistent Saved and always restored. Persistent objects include: Important NPCs (a lost traveller lives in their quest record) Quest locations and boss locations (cleared places stay cleared) Major discoveries and special landmarks Important loot: chests never refill, and items on the ground are saved. Legends' drops (schematics, star metal) go into the bag, or onto the ground when it is full. 5. Gameplay systems These systems sit on top of the world technology and are built after the MVP. System Features Player Walking, running, dodge rolls, swimming. Health, stamina, hunger, thirst. Damage and collapse: the player wakes at the last rest spot (a campfire, a bed, a Moonwell, or the last story stage reached). Status effects and equipment. No jumping or crouching in the top-down view. Combat Melee and ranged weapons, projectiles. Dodge, block, critical hits. Damage types (frost, venom, spirit): each species can be weak to one (×1.5) or resist it (×0.5). Armor, weapon durability. Enemy drops and boss fights. Enemies Biome-based spawning and despawning. AI states: patrol, aggro, chase, attack, flee. Group behaviour, difficulty scaling, elites, variants drawn from existing sprites, a legendary boss per land. No wild spawns within 64 tiles of the origin (safe zone). Dungeons wake skeletons, skeleton archers and, further out, ghouls; boss arenas wake an Arena Guardian. Resources Wood, stone, iron, coal, gold, rare minerals, herbs, food, water, biome-specific resources. Crafting Recipes, crafting stations, crafting several at once (up to 20). Tiers: wood and stone, iron, then starforged (star ingots from star metal and gold). Rare recipes stay hidden until a Forgotten Schematic teaches them. Produces tools, weapons, armor and consumables. Stations are found in the world, never built: campfires in camps, anvils in villages, castles and nomad camps. Inventory Stacking and quantities, equipment slots, hotbar, a Sort bag button (by category, rarest first), rarity, weight limits, dropping and picking up. A full bag leaves the rest on the ground. Progression XP and levels (cap 50). 9 passive skills of 5 ranks each; no active abilities. Equipment, crafting and exploration progression. Legends defeated are kept as trophies. Difficulty scaling. One rule ties these systems back to the world: Resource rarity depends on biome, terrain, depth, distance from origin and randomness. There is no building system: the player is an adventurer, not a settler. 6. Living world Only the area near the player is simulated. Distant entities get no full simulation. 6.1 Time and weather System Features Day/night cycle Sunrise, day, sunset, night. Darkness, torches and light sources. Night-only enemies, structures and events. NPC schedules. Weather Rain, storms, snow, blizzards, fog, sandstorms, heat waves. Lighting changes, environmental hazards. Temperature has no bar of its own: it is folded into how fast hunger and thirst drain, by biome and weather. A full day lasts 16 real minutes. Players can rest by a campfire, or in a bed indoors, at dusk or night to skip to morning. Wraiths roam only at night, more enemies spawn after dark, and lit windows, campfires, torches and lanterns push back the darkness. Weather depends on the biome and feeds into gameplay. Each biome type runs its own weather pattern (forest: rain, thunderstorms, fog; desert: sandstorms, heat waves; snow: snow, blizzards, fog), and the player feels the weather of the biome they stand in. Thunderstorms bring lightning strikes that can set trees burning. Fire spreads to nearby plants, far less in wet weather (5% per neighbour instead of 35%). Biome → Weather → Environment → Gameplay 6.2 Simulation Simulated within the simulation radius: NPC, animal and enemy behaviour (villagers wander by day and go indoors at night) Resource regeneration (berry bushes, herbs and mushrooms regrow after a day or so while their region stays loaded) Weather and fire Environmental and time-based events 6.3 Quests and world events Type Examples Generated quests Collect resources, find rare items (rarer further out), slay enemies, discover a place, clear a dungeon, rescue and escort a lost traveller, defend the village Random world events Meteor crash, bandit attack, monster invasion, treasure event, rare merchant, ancient structure appearing, a Moonwell at night Quests come from villagers: each village has three villagers (one offering a new quest each day) and a trader. A nomad camp has one quest giver and a trader. Defend quests are only offered in villages and start an invasion at once; they complete when it is driven off. Quests complete on their own when the objective is met, except collect and find quests, which are handed in to any villager. Unfinished quests can be abandoned. Rewards are coins, experience and an item. Coins buy goods from traders and travelling merchants, who also buy treasure. A lost traveller is an important NPC: their position lives in the quest record, so they persist until rescued, while ordinary villagers come and go with their village. 6.4 Exploration Map with fog of war, coordinates and compass Discovered locations and landmarks Waypoints and player markers Rare discoveries and exploration rewards 7. Presentation The theme, UI and visual design follow Stardew Valley. All art, audio and UI assets are original work made in that style, not copies of the game's own assets. 7.1 Visual direction Aspect Direction Perspective Top-down, three-quarter view Art style 16-bit style pixel art on a square tile grid Palette Warm and saturated, with a distinct palette per biome Tone Cozy and inviting near the origin, more threatening with distance Characters Small, expressive sprites with four-direction walk animations UI style Wood-framed panels, pixel font, tabbed menus 7.2 HUD layout Element Position Compass Top left; points to the story goal or the tracked marker Location panel: clock, day, weather, biome, difficulty, position Top right Quest tracker Top right, under the location panel Health, stamina, hunger and thirst bars Bottom right, vertical Level and XP, status effects Bottom right, above the vitals Notifications and tips Bottom left Action prompt Above the hotbar Hotbar Bottom centre Boss bar and low-vital warnings Top centre Alerts that stay until dismissed Middle Main menu Full-screen panel with tabs: Inventory, Crafting, Skills, Quests, Map, Lantern, Settings, About On touch screens the vitals, level and status effects sit under the compass, and the buttons sit bottom right: Use, Bag, Hit, Roll and Block, with small Pause and Full buttons above them. 7.3 Features Area Features Rendering 2D tiles and sprites, animated sprites, animation system. Particles, lighting, shadows, weather and screen effects. Camera movement and zoom. No parallax backgrounds: they do not fit the top-down view. Audio Background music with a voice and tempo per biome, lower and darker further out. Weather sounds, birds by day, crickets at night, wind in the desert and snow, drips and a drone in mines. Footsteps, combat sounds, and calls (howls, wails, growls) from creatures hunting the player. UI Health, stamina, hunger and thirst bars. Hotbar, inventory, crafting menu, map, quest UI. Interaction prompts, notifications and first-time tips. Settings (key rebinding, text and HUD size, screen flashes) and pause menu. 8. Technical design The game runs in the browser. Generation happens off the main thread so streaming never stalls the frame. 8.1 Tech stack The stack is chosen for frame rate first and for running on low-end phones and laptops. Layer Choice Why Language TypeScript Type safety across the main thread and worker boundary Build tool Vite Fast rebuilds and small production bundles Renderer PixiJS v8, WebGL renderer Batched GPU sprite drawing that runs on older devices Tile drawing One batched mesh per chunk, one texture atlas Very few draw calls per frame World generation Web Workers, typed arrays sent as transferable buffers Generation never blocks a frame and chunk data is not copied Noise Seeded simplex noise Cheap to compute and repeatable per seed Saves IndexedDB Local, asynchronous, no server needed Audio Web Audio API Built into every browser, no extra download Menus HTML and CSS overlay Costs nothing while closed and keeps the canvas for the world PixiJS is preferred over a full framework such as Phaser because the world systems here are custom anyway, and a renderer-only library is lighter. WebGPU stays off by default: the PixiJS rendering docs recommend the WebGL renderer for production. Rules for low-end devices: Render at a low internal resolution and scale up with nearest-neighbour filtering. Pixel art loses nothing and the GPU fills far fewer pixels. Cap the device pixel ratio at 1. Give lighting, shadows, particles and weather effects a low-quality setting that turns them off. Offer a 30 fps cap. Pool chunks and entities so the garbage collector rarely runs during play. 8.2 Threading As built, the worker generates terrain tiles and decorations only. Structures, lakes and events are rolled on the main thread, because they need the live object list to avoid collisions. MAIN THREAD WEB WORKERS (1 to 3) ├── Game loop └── Terrain tiles and decorations ├── Biomes, structures, lakes, events └── Renderer (WebGL) 8.3 Performance techniques Area Techniques Streaming Chunk streaming, generation prioritisation, lazy loading, render-distance and simulation-distance management Concurrency Web Workers, background generation Memory Chunk pooling, object pooling, typed arrays, asset caching Rendering WebGL, sprite batching, one texture atlas, chunk culling to the camera view, separate render groups for ground and entities, pooled display objects, at most 2 chunks drawn per frame 8.4 Save system Saves stay small because most of the world is never stored. Saves live in IndexedDB. A save contains: World seed Player state, inventory and progression Persistent objects Important discoveries 8.5 Project layout The source layout as built is described in docs/architecture.md. 9. Roadmap Build the world technology first. It is the unique part of the game and the riskiest, so it should be proven before combat, crafting or quests. 9.1 MVP build order Player movement and camera Chunk streaming Procedural terrain Biomes Procedural structures Multi-chunk object lifetime Regeneration of discarded areas The MVP is done when a multi-chunk structure survives a partial unload and the area regenerates differently after a full unload. 9.2 After the MVP The original notes only fixed the MVP. The phases below are now built. The current behaviour of every system is documented in docs/. Phase Goal Built 2. Persistence The world remembers what matters Persistence tiers, IndexedDB saves with versioned upgrades 3. Survival loop There is a reason to explore Resources, inventory, crafting, vitals, swimming 4. Threat The world pushes back Enemies and wildlife, combat, distance-based difficulty, progression, legends, damage types, the starforged tier 5. Living world The world feels alive Day/night, weather, hazards, NPCs, quests, trade, world events, rare events, the story 6. Polish The game is presentable Map and exploration tools, audio, lighting, particles, full UI, accessibility settings, touch controls, key rebinding Not built: Building: dropped by design. The player is an adventurer. Jumping, crouching and parallax backgrounds: dropped; they do not fit the top-down view. A separate underground layer: mines and keep cellars are interiors entered through buildings instead. A temperature bar: temperature is folded into hunger and thirst drain. Multiplayer and accounts. A smaller Pixi bundle without the WebGPU code: Pixi's main export imports it statically, so splitting it out needs a custom Pixi build. vite.config.ts is unchanged. 10. Open questions These gaps surfaced while tightening the notes. The first three, plus chunk size and minimum biome size, must be settled before the MVP. Side-view leftovers. The Stardew Valley direction makes the game top-down. Jumping, crouching and parallax backgrounds do not fit that view. Drop them or redefine them? Decided: dropped. The dodge roll and swimming cover movement instead. How regeneration differs. A seeded RNG gives the same result for the same seed and coordinates. Regenerating differently needs an extra input, such as a per-region visit counter or world time. Decided: a visit counter per 8 × 8-chunk region, raised when the region fully leaves the cache radius. Rivers and mountains stay fixed; biomes, ground detail, decorations, lakes and structures vary. Biome regeneration. A discarded biome can currently return as a different type. Confirm this, or fix the type per area and vary only size, shape and contents. Decided: the type is fixed per area; size, shape, variant and contents vary. Semi-persistent tier. What does it keep, and for how long? Decided: villages, castles, mines and nomad camps are semi-persistent. Once discovered, one is kept unchanged for 3 in-game days after the player last left it, then forgotten like a temporary place. Player changes to temporary objects. Does a looted chest or cleared dungeon reset when its area regenerates? Decided: chests never refill. A looted chest stays open, and chests that later regenerate from the same anchor start empty. Gathered trees, rocks and plants come back only when their region regenerates. Cleared dungeons, defeated arena bosses and slain legendary creatures stay cleared the same way. Map and waypoints. What does the map show for a discovered location that has since regenerated? Decided by the story: the lantern remembers it. Forgotten places are listed in the Lantern tab and show as ghosts in the lantern's light. Rare event odds. Are the percentages per chunk, per region or per structure roll? Decided: per chunk. The legendary creature rolls once per chunk per region visit, at 0.05% × (1 + band). Chunk size, simulation radius and cache radius. No values are set yet. Decided: 32 × 32 tiles, simulation radius 2 chunks, cache radius = render area + 2 chunks. Minimum biome size. The value of the constant is not set yet. Decided: 64 chunks. Persistent objects in a regenerated biome. A persistent landmark is saved but its biome is not. What happens if the area comes back as a different biome? Resolved: biome types are fixed per area, and there is no player building. "},{"slug":"architecture","title":"Architecture","group":"Engine","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"source-layout","text":"Source layout"},{"level":2,"id":"the-frame","text":"The frame"},{"level":2,"id":"threads","text":"Threads"},{"level":2,"id":"how-systems-talk","text":"How systems talk"},{"level":2,"id":"testing","text":"Testing"}],"text":"Architecture How the source is organised, what runs each frame, and how the parts talk to each other. Source layout Path Contents src/main.ts Entry point. Creates Game; dev builds expose it as window.game. src/config.ts World constants: tile and chunk size, margins, minimum biome size, region size, speeds. src/coords.ts Chunk keys, region keys and chunk rectangles. src/style.css All HTML UI styling. src/game/ The game shell. Game.ts Boots PixiJS, owns the frame loop, wires every system together, spawning and saving. LivingWorld.ts Owns time, weather, hazards, NPCs, quests, events, visions, lighting and weather rendering, and the story (RiftStory), which plugs in here. Place.ts Place: where the player is (the overworld, an Interior, or a RiftTrip): the player's collision, the combat Arena, and the player's spot in the overworld. RiftTrip.ts One trip into an outer dimension: the run, the enemies it asked for, the map's fog, and the dimension as a Place and combat Arena. Player.ts Movement, collision, dodge roll, knockback and invulnerability. Camera.ts Smooth follow and zoom (0.25 to 2). Input.ts Keyboard, mouse, wheel, the touch stick (left half), pinch zoom and tap-to-interact. Game code asks for actions (act, holding), not keys. Keys.ts Key bindings: each action's main key (DEFAULT_KEYS, changeable in Settings), fixed extras such as the arrow keys, RESERVED_KEYS (Esc, F3, F4, 1–8) and key names for the screen. Settings.ts Player settings kept in localStorage. src/world/ The streamed world, independent of rendering. WorldManager.ts Ties streaming, biomes, objects, regions and the harvest log together. ChunkManager.ts Visible, render, cache and simulation areas; generation queue; chunk lifecycle. Chunk.ts Chunk buffers and the chunk pool. GroundItems.ts Items lying on the ground, picked up by walking over them, saved with the world. Interior.ts Building interiors, mine caves and the keep's floors, generated from the building's seed; the bookshelves' books. BiomeManager.ts Biome records and chunk ownership. ObjectManager.ts Live world objects indexed by chunk and by kind. WorldObject.ts One object spanning one or more chunks, and how it stamps onto a chunk. Regions.ts Per-region visit counters that drive regeneration. SyncGenerator.ts Main-thread chunk generator for tests. src/generation/ Pure, seeded generation code. Runs in workers and on the main thread. TerrainGenerator.ts Noise fields (TerrainFields) and generateChunk. BiomeGenerator.ts Biome footprint growth. StructureGenerator.ts Layout, structure layouts, the structure catalogue and the per-chunk roll. EnemyGenerator.ts Enemy spawn rules. Noise.ts Seeded 2D simplex noise. Random.ts Hashing, the Rng class and named random streams. Tiles.ts Tile, decoration and biome ids; solidity. WorldRules.ts Difficulty bands by distance. src/workers/ worldGenerator.worker.ts (runs generateChunk) and WorkerGenerator.ts (the worker pool). src/dimension/ The outer dimensions behind the rifts, with no rendering: DimTile.ts (their tiles), Dimension.ts (what a dimension is, and the registry), Hollow.ts (The Hollow and its generator), Challenges.ts (the challenge state machines and DimensionRun). See dimensions.md. src/story/ RiftStory.ts: the main story, its visions, objective and save. See story.md. src/entities/ Enemy.ts (enemy AI), Species.ts (enemy roster, damage-type weaknesses and resistances, variants), NPC.ts (NPC schedules and following). src/items/ Items.ts: the item catalogue. src/systems/ Gameplay systems: inventory, crafting, gathering, loot, vitals, progression, combat, NPCs, quests, trade, time, weather, hazards, world events, exploration. src/rendering/ Atlas.ts, TileRenderer.ts, EntityRenderer.ts, Buildings.ts, InteriorRenderer.ts, DimensionRenderer.ts, GroundItemRenderer.ts, Lighting.ts, ParticleSystem.ts, Effects.ts, RiftGlitch.ts (the world's glitches near a rift). src/ui/ HTML UI: Hud, Hotbar, Menu (tabs: Inventory, Crafting, Skills, Quests, Map (only inside an outer dimension), Settings, About), MapView, DimensionMap, PauseMenu, Dialog, Vision, SleepOverlay, DebugOverlay, TouchStick, ItemIcon, Hints (first-time tips), Fullscreen (the Fullscreen API), About (privacy, credits, licenses), Playground (the story playground panel). src/audio/ AudioSystem.ts: synthesised sound effects, generated music and weather ambience; see audio.md. src/save/ SaveManager.ts: IndexedDB saves and migration. Tests sit next to the code they cover as *.test.ts. The frame Game.tick(dt) runs once per frame, with dt capped at 0.1 seconds: handleKeys(): debug overlay, menu, vision paging, Escape, save, zoom, hotbar, use, interact, attack, dodge and the debug far trip. If the player is waiting to be placed (spawnTarget), trySpawn() looks for open ground once the chunks around the target have loaded. Indoors, tickIndoors; in an outer dimension, tickDimension (the clock, needs and villagers run as indoors; the player moves on the dimension, combat fights on it, and the run is stepped and its events acted on; see dimensions.md). Otherwise, unless a menu, dialog or vision pauses the world: LivingWorld.update(dt): clock and hourly events, hazards, NPCs, lost travellers and escorts. Blocking state and movement speed (slow status, blocking, weather). Player.update, then gathering, then CombatSystem.update with the player's body. The camera follows the player. Vitals.update; a collapse sends the player to their waking spot (the last rest or Moonwell; the origin before any). WorldManager.update(view) with the camera's view in tiles (indoors and in a dimension, a view around the place's spot outside, so the world there stays loaded): streaming, biome and object bookkeeping, discovery. ChunkManager draws at most 2 waiting chunks, nearest first. The world layer is positioned and scaled for the camera. Indoors, the room renders; in a dimension, DimensionRenderer culls and animates, then entities and the dimension's lighting. Outdoors, TileRenderer.cull() hides chunks outside the camera's view, rifts animate, the glitches run near an open rift, and buildings and ground items near the screen update. updateTarget(): the interaction prompt and target outline. Rendering updates: tree fading, EntityRenderer.update, then LivingWorld.render (weather particles, effects, lighting), and the music mood (biome, band, indoors or cave, what is hunting the player). Touch stick, hotbar and menu refresh. Autosave every 30 seconds; every 0.25 seconds, the HUD refresh and first-time tips. Input.endFrame() clears one-frame inputs. The ticker is capped at 60 frames per second (30 with the setting). Threads Terrain generation runs off the main thread so streaming never stalls a frame. ChunkManager talks to a ChunkGenerator: request(req) and onResult(handler). WorkerGenerator runs between 1 and 3 web workers (one fewer than navigator.hardwareConcurrency, capped at 3) and hands out requests round-robin. Each worker builds its own TerrainFields for the seed and returns tiles and decorations as transferable Uint8Array buffers, so nothing is copied. SyncGenerator runs generateChunk on the calling thread and answers immediately. Tests use it. Only terrain tiles and decorations come from the workers. Biome records, structure rolls, object stamping, enemies and everything else run on the main thread. Structure rolls use the same TerrainFields (for flat-ground checks), built on the main thread. How systems talk Systems hold references to what they need and report back through callbacks: Hook Raised by Used for WorldManager.onDiscover(o) The player first stands inside an object Discovery toast, XP, quest discovery objectives ChunkListener.show / hide / changed ChunkManager TileRenderer builds, removes and refreshes chunk meshes CombatSystem.onKill(enemy) An enemy dies Quest slay objectives CombatSystem.onCleared(kind) A guarded group is wiped out Quest clear objectives, village defence reward Arena.killed(enemy) An enemy dies on an arena (a dimension) DimensionRun.enemyKilled DimensionRun.takeEvents() Each frame of a trip RiftTrip spawns and removes enemies and scales the boss; Game.tripEvent moves the player after a fall, grants rewards, shows notices, closes the rift or leaves QuestSystem.onComplete(q) A quest finishes Rewards CombatEvents, HazardHooks, EventHooks Their systems Damage numbers, swings, hurt flashes, notifications, visions, dialogs WorldManager.now is a function the game clock provides; the world uses it to timestamp harvests for regrowth. Testing Vitest runs headless. WorldManager does not touch the renderer, so tests build a world with a SyncGenerator and drive it with world.update(view) and hand-made views. The suites cover: File Covers src/world/WorldManager.test.ts Streaming, minimum biome size, bounded memory, determinism, multi-chunk lifetime, regeneration src/world/Buildings.test.ts Buildings, interiors, castles, discovery and forgetting, saves that keep the world as it was src/world/Rivers.test.ts Rivers and bridges, the spawn, mines src/save/SaveManager.test.ts Save round trips and migrating older saves src/entities/NPC.test.ts NPCs at night src/systems/Survival.test.ts Inventory, crafting, vitals, gathering, chests that never refill src/systems/Adventurer.test.ts Carrying, skills, places forgetting and the object indexes, the Moonwell, equipment src/systems/Progress.test.ts Sorting the bag, selling a stack, damage types, legends and trophies, rare recipes and the star tier, abandoning and defending in quests, key bindings src/systems/Combat.test.ts Damage, enemy AI, spawning, weapons, dungeons, progression src/systems/LivingWorld.test.ts Time, weather, regrowth, NPCs, quests, trade, events, hazards src/systems/Exploration.test.ts Fog of war, milestones, markers src/dimension/Dimension.test.ts, Challenges.test.ts, DimensionRenderer.test.ts The Hollow's layout, every challenge and the run, the dimension renderer's culling and pooling src/game/RiftTrip.test.ts Rift structures (placement, determinism, closing), trips (stashing and restoring the overworld's fights, kills and boss phases, closing through the portal), saving inside, dying inside, the story's objectives See also: World streaming, Rendering "},{"slug":"world-streaming","title":"World streaming","group":"Engine","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"chunks","text":"Chunks"},{"level":2,"id":"areas","text":"Areas"},{"level":2,"id":"lifecycle","text":"Lifecycle"},{"level":2,"id":"the-generation-queue","text":"The generation queue"},{"level":2,"id":"stale-results","text":"Stale results"},{"level":2,"id":"pooling","text":"Pooling"},{"level":2,"id":"composition","text":"Composition"}],"text":"World streaming How chunks are generated around the player, kept while nearby, and thrown away behind them. Chunks The world is a grid of chunks that extends in every direction. Constant Value Where TILE_SIZE 16 px src/config.ts CHUNK_SIZE 32 × 32 tiles src/config.ts CHUNK_PIXELS 512 px src/config.ts A chunk key packs (cx, cy) into one safe integer: (cx + 2^23) × 2^24 + (cy + 2^23) (chunkKey in src/coords.ts). That allows about ±8 million chunks on each axis. keyX and keyY unpack it, and tileToChunk floors a tile coordinate to its chunk. Areas ChunkManager.update(view) works out four rectangles of chunks from the camera's view (in tiles) and the player's position: Area Rule Constant Visible Chunks the view touches Render Visible, plus a margin on every side RENDER_MARGIN = 1 Cache Render, plus a margin on every side CACHE_MARGIN = 2 Simulation The player's chunk, plus a radius SIM_RADIUS = 2 The render area grows and shrinks with the screen size and zoom, so the player never sees a chunk appear at the edge. The cache area always covers the render area. A cache margin of at least 1 is required: structures can reach one chunk out from their anchor, so every object that touches the render area has its anchor chunk inside the cache area and is rolled before it is drawn. Lifecycle Step What happens Where Predict The render area is extended by one chunk in the direction of travel. ChunkManager.schedule Generate Missing chunks in that area are queued and sent to the generator. ChunkManager.schedule Activate A finished chunk inside the render area is queued to be shown (pendingShow); at most 2 are shown per frame (SHOW_PER_FRAME), nearest to the player first. Inside the simulation area it is marked simulated. ChunkManager.receive, syncShown, flushShows Deactivate Leaving the render area hides it (or drops it from the queue); leaving the simulation area clears simulated. ChunkManager.update Dispose Leaving the cache area removes it and returns it to the pool. ChunkManager.setCache When the cache rectangle changes, setCache reports the chunks that left (cacheLeave) and the chunks that entered (cacheEnter). WorldManager uses these to keep biome records, objects and region visit counters up to date (see World generation and Objects and structures). Only chunks in the render area plus the predicted strip are generated; the rest of the cache area is tracked (biomes, objects, regions) without terrain. The generation queue Each frame, schedule() collects the chunks in the predicted area that have no Chunk yet and scores them: score = distance from the player's chunk − 0.5 × (offset · direction of travel) + (100 if outside the render area) Lower scores go first, so chunks inside the render area come before the predicted strip, nearer chunks before farther ones, and chunks ahead before chunks behind. At most MAX_IN_FLIGHT (6) requests are outstanding at once. Stale results Each request carries a token from a running counter, also stored on the Chunk. When a result arrives, receive() always decrements the in-flight count, then drops the result if the chunk was disposed or reused in the meantime (chunk.token !== res.token). Pooling ChunkPool keeps disposed Chunk objects and their four 1,024-byte buffers for reuse, so streaming rarely allocates. The renderer pools its meshes and decoration sprites the same way (see Rendering). Composition A chunk keeps two copies of its data: baseTiles and baseDecos: what the worker generated. tiles and decos: what the game shows and collides with. WorldManager's compose hook rebuilds the shown copy: Copy the base terrain. Stamp every live object that overlaps the chunk, in the order they were added (WorldObject.stamp). Remove decorations the player harvested, if the harvest record is from the current region visit and the plant has not grown back. ChunkManager.refresh(chunk) re-runs composition and tells the renderer when an object is added, removed or changed, a chest is opened, or something is harvested. WorldManager.tileAt(gx, gy) reads the composed data. It returns null for chunks that are not generated, and isSolidAt treats those as solid so the player cannot walk into unloaded ground. isSolidAt is called many times a frame by every walker, so it reads the chunk's arrays directly instead of building a tile object. Spreading the shows out keeps a frame from stalling when several results land at once. Without a renderer listening (tests and tools), chunks are marked shown at once. The renderer also culls shown chunks outside the camera's view (see Rendering). See also: World generation, Objects and structures, Architecture "},{"slug":"world-generation","title":"World generation","group":"Engine","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"seeds-and-streams","text":"Seeds and streams"},{"level":2,"id":"noise-fields","text":"Noise fields"},{"level":2,"id":"tiles","text":"Tiles"},{"level":3,"id":"trails","text":"Trails"},{"level":3,"id":"bridges","text":"Bridges"},{"level":3,"id":"marshes","text":"Marshes"},{"level":2,"id":"decorations","text":"Decorations"},{"level":2,"id":"biomes","text":"Biomes"},{"level":2,"id":"regions-and-regeneration","text":"Regions and regeneration"},{"level":2,"id":"world-scale-rules","text":"World-scale rules"}],"text":"World generation How terrain, decorations and biomes are made from a seed, and how areas regenerate differently. Seeds and streams Everything comes from the world seed. src/generation/Random.ts provides: hash(...values) and hash3(a, b, c): integer mixing hashes. hash3 is allocation-free for per-tile loops. Rng: a Mulberry32 generator with int, range, chance, pick, weighted and uint. Stream: named streams (Noise, Biome, Decoration, Structure, Lake, RareEvent, Layout, Loot). streamRng(stream, ...values) seeds an Rng from the stream id plus the values, so each system rolls independently and changing one never shifts another. Generation is deterministic: the same seed and the same visit counters give the same world (see is repeatable for the same seed and path in src/world/WorldManager.test.ts). Noise fields TerrainFields (src/generation/TerrainGenerator.ts) builds eleven seeded simplex noise fields (src/generation/Noise.ts), each seeded with hash(Stream.Noise, seed, n). Each field is also shifted by a seeded offset, so the origin is not a noise grid point (there simplex noise is always 0 and its contours run in straight lines): Field Used for height Hills and mountains: fbm(gx/200, gy/200, 4 octaves) erosion Lowers high ground: heightAt subtracts max(0, erosion(gx/600)) × 0.35; also adds to moisture. Near the spawn heightAt also subtracts up to 0.6 (fully within 48 tiles of the origin, fading out by 88), so no mountain ever boxes the player in. river River centrelines: the zero line of fbm(wx/450, wy/450, 2) at a warped position riverGate Where rivers exist at all (scale 900) and their width (scale 260) temperature Climate, per chunk (scale 26 chunks) moisture Climate, per chunk (scale 22 chunks) warp Biome border blending, roughening biome footprints, river meanders and trail bends detail Ground patches, decoration density, per-biome offsets trail Trail centrelines: the zero lines of two warped fields (see Trails) trailGate Where each trail network exists zone Landscape zones (scale 80): meadows, deep woods, dune seas, open snowfields; marshes (scale 150); and rock fields (scale 22) The same class runs in the workers (tile generation) and on the main thread (biome climate and structure ground checks). Tiles generateChunk picks one tile per cell, in this order: Test Tile On a river (dist < width) and below MOUNTAIN_HIGH (0.62) Snow biomes: Ice (frozen, walkable). Otherwise WaterDeep near the centreline (dist < width × 0.45), else Water. On a trail, and not above MOUNTAIN_HIGH Path (so trails cross low rock as passes, but go round peaks) Height above MOUNTAIN (0.52) RockDark above 0.62, else Rock Within width + 1.2 tiles of a river The palette's shore tile Otherwise A ground tile from the biome palette Rivers (riverAt): Meanders: the river field is sampled at a warped position (warp noise at scale 170, ±46 tiles, plus scale 50, ±6 tiles), so rivers bend instead of following the noise grid. Even width: dist is the field's value divided by its slope (one-tile finite differences), which is roughly the distance in tiles to the centreline. The half-width is 2.6 + 1.2 × riverGate(gx/260) tiles, so rivers are about 3–8 tiles wide. Ends: width is scaled by a fade that rises smoothly as the gate noise goes from −0.15 to 0.15, so rivers narrow to a point instead of stopping square. Below 0.8 tiles there is no water. Dry spawn: the fade is also 0 within 36 tiles of the origin and rises over the next 40, so the player always wakes on dry land. Trails Two trail networks wind across the world, each the zero line of its own trail field, found the same way as river centrelines (value over slope): Network Field scale Half-width Exists where trailGate is above Main trails 260 1.05 tiles −0.3 (scale 700) Footpaths 140 0.55 tiles 0.2 (scale 420) Bends: the field is sampled at a position warped by warp at scale 90 (±14 tiles). Spawn: within 150 tiles of the origin the main field is shifted by its own value at the origin (fading out over the next 250), and its gate is held open, so a main trail always runs through the spawn. Worn edges: a tile within 0.6 tiles of a trail's edge is also Path 30% of the time (hashed from the seed and position). Clear of trees: no tall decoration within a tile of a trail. Trails depend only on the seed, like rivers. Cost: generateChunk samples the river and trail fields once per tile corner (shared between neighbouring tiles), checks trail fields first on a 4-tile grid, and skips the slope where the value shows the trail is far away. Trail widths are blended from the chunk's corners. The biome border warp is skipped where no neighbouring chunk within reach has a different biome. Bridges Plank bridges cross rivers (TerrainFields.bridgeAt). The world is split into cells of 80 × 80 tiles, and each cell has an 85% chance of a bridge: The cell's middle (8 tiles in from each edge) is sampled every 3 tiles for river centreline points (dist < 1.2, half-width at least 1.5, not near mountains). The point where the river runs closest to straight north–south or east–west wins (the river's slope direction is at least 85% along one axis), so the bridge crosses square. Points where a trail crosses (within about a tile) score 0.6 higher and need only 72%, so the bridge carries the trail; a cell with a trail crossing always gets a bridge. The bridge is two tiles wide and runs across the river: from the site it walks out along both rows until it reaches dry ground (anything that is not water or shore). Bridges longer than 22 tiles are dropped. generateChunk turns water, ice and shore tiles under a bridge into BridgeH/BridgeH2 (running east–west, rails on the north and south sides) or BridgeV/BridgeV2 (running north–south, rails on the west and east sides). Bridges are worked out per cell and cached (up to 4096 cells), and a tile checks the cells around its own, so a bridge can reach past its cell. Like rivers, they depend only on the seed. Players can also swim; see survival.md. Each palette (biome type × variant) has four ground kinds: Base, Alt, Detail and Shore. Tile id = palette × 4 + kind; shared tiles (water, rock, floors, walls, roofs and so on) start at id 24. Ground kind comes from detail noise at scale 11, offset by the biome's seed: Forest and snow: Alt where the noise is above 0.38 − open × 0.3, Detail below −0.64, else Base. Desert: dune stripes (sin(gx × 0.11 + gy × 0.04 + d × 3) > 0.8 − deep × 0.35) are Alt, Detail below −0.6, else Base. open and deep come from the zone field z at scale 80: open = smooth((−z − 0.25) / 0.25), deep = smooth((z − 0.3) / 0.25). Marshes Marshes are a forest landscape zone, kept more than 200 tiles from the start (MARSH_MIN_DISTANCE). marsh = smooth((z − 0.4) / 0.15), with z the zone field at scale 150 (sampled at its own offset). Where marsh is above 0, on forest ground: Pools: a tile becomes shallow Water where detail at scale 6 is above 0.8 − marsh × 0.62, so pools grow thicker toward the marsh's heart. No pool forms within the river's width + 8 tiles, so bridges always land on dry ground. Darker ground: elsewhere, the Detail ground kind is used where the ground noise is below −0.3 + marsh × 0.4. Plants: see the forest row under Decorations. The six palettes are Forest, Autumn Forest, Desert, Red Desert, Snowfield and Crystal Tundra (BIOME_NAMES in src/generation/Tiles.ts). Decorations Decorations are rolled per tile on ground tiles only (not water, rock, shared tiles or shore), with unit(hash3(decoSeed, lx, ly)). decoSeed = hash(Stream.Decoration, seed, cx, cy, visit), so a regenerated region gets new decorations. A smooth density field (detail at scale 45, offset by the biome seed, clamped at 0) clusters trees into groves. Biome Roll thresholds (cumulative) Forest Trees ((0.01 + density × 0.17) × (1 − open × 0.95) + deep × 0.22) × (1 − marsh × 0.75), × 0.4 in rock fields (18% + deep × 30% pines, none in marshes, otherwise oaks or autumn oaks), then bush +0.02 + marsh × 0.06 (+0.05 instead of 0.02 near rivers), dead bush +marsh × 0.05, flowers +0.015 + open × 0.07 (+0.02 near rivers), mushroom +0.007 + deep × 0.025 + marsh × 0.03, stone +0.004 (+0.05 in rock fields), ore +0.0015 (+0.006 in rock fields) Desert Palms +0.09 near rivers (oases), cactus (0.008 + density × 0.03) × (1 + open × 3) × (1 − deep × 0.8), dead bush +0.012, rock +0.006 (+0.06 in rock fields; red rock in the red variant, half red in rock fields), ore +0.0015 (+0.005 in rock fields) Snow Snowy pines (0.01 + density × 0.11) × (1 − open × 0.9) + deep × 0.14, snow rock +0.008 (+0.05 in rock fields), crystal +0.012 in the crystal variant or +0.0015 otherwise, ore +0.0015 (+0.005 in rock fields) So forests have flowery meadows, dark deep woods and, further out, marshes thick with bushes, reeds (dead brush) and mushrooms but few trees; deserts have cactus stands, bare dune seas and palm oases, and snow has open snowfields between stands of taiga. Rock fields (zone at scale 22 above 0.5) are scattered in every biome. \"Near rivers\" means within width + 5 tiles. Tall decorations (oaks, pines, palms, cacti) are wider than a tile, so one is dropped if another tall one already sits left, above, above-left or above-right of it. Biomes A biome is a record that owns many chunks (BiomeRecord in src/world/BiomeManager.ts): type, variant, a seed, its chunk keys and how many of them are in the cache area. Type. TerrainFields.climateAt(cx, cy) reads temperature (with a north-south latitude bias of up to ±0.4, lower on high ground, plus a little per-chunk randomness) and moisture. Below −0.28 is Snow; above 0.15 with moisture below 0.05 is Desert; everything else is Forest. Chunks within 6 of the origin on both axes are always Forest: the safe starting zone. Climate depends only on the seed, so an area keeps its type when it regenerates. Creation. When a chunk enters the cache area with no owner, BiomeManager.ensure makes a new biome there. It rolls a seed and a variant (8% chance of the rare variant: Autumn Forest, Red Desert or Crystal Tundra) from streamRng(Stream.Biome, seed, cx, cy, visit), then grows a footprint with growBiome: A target size between MIN_BIOME_CHUNKS (64) and 192 chunks. A rotated, stretched ellipse around the trigger chunk, roughened by warp noise, sets each candidate's priority. Free chunks are claimed nearest-first through a min-heap. Chunks whose climate disagrees with the type are pushed to the back and only taken to reach the minimum size. Merging. If loaded neighbours hem the new biome in below 64 chunks, its chunks join the neighbouring biome it touches most, so there are no slivers. Lifetime. A biome is dropped when none of its chunks remain in the cache area. A later visit grows a new one, usually with different borders, size, variant and seed. Border blending. generateChunk receives the type, variant and seed of the 3 × 3 chunks around it. Each tile looks up its biome at a warped position (two octaves of warp noise, up to about 14 tiles of offset), so borders are irregular rather than chunk-aligned. Regions and regeneration The world is divided into regions of 8 × 8 chunks (REGION_SIZE). RegionTracker (src/world/Regions.ts) counts each region's chunks in the cache area and keeps a visit counter. When a region's last cached chunk leaves, its visit counter goes up, if the current rules allow it (see below). Every roll that should change on a new visit includes the region's visit counter: biome records, decorations and structures. Chunks that unload while other chunks of their region are still cached regenerate identically, so nothing changes under the player's feet. Rivers, mountains and climate use only the seed, so landforms stay put while the biomes, plants and structures on them change. Every region that fully unloads regenerates: its visit counter always rises. World-scale rules src/generation/WorldRules.ts sets difficulty by straight-line distance from the origin, in tiles: Band Distance Easy 0 to 1,000 Medium 1,000 to 5,000 Hard 5,000 to 10,000 Extreme 10,000 and beyond The band raises the odds of rarer structure tiers (see Objects and structures), and rare structures have minimum distances, so rarer things appear farther out. Enemies and legendary creatures scale with the band too (see Combat), ore and crystal give rarer finds (Survival), and the light and music grow grimmer (Living world, Audio). See also: World streaming, Objects and structures "},{"slug":"objects-and-structures","title":"Objects and structures","group":"Engine","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"layouts","text":"Layouts"},{"level":2,"id":"world-objects","text":"World objects"},{"level":2,"id":"the-roll","text":"The roll"},{"level":2,"id":"placement","text":"Placement"},{"level":2,"id":"structure-catalogue","text":"Structure catalogue"},{"level":2,"id":"rifts","text":"Rifts"},{"level":2,"id":"lakes","text":"Lakes"},{"level":2,"id":"chests-cleared-places-and-harvesting","text":"Chests, cleared places and harvesting"},{"level":2,"id":"other-placed-objects","text":"Other placed objects"}],"text":"Objects and structures How structures, lakes and other placed things are built, rolled, kept alive and forgotten. Layouts A Layout (src/generation/StructureGenerator.ts) is a rectangle of two byte arrays stamped over the terrain: Array Value Meaning tiles 0 Keep the terrain's tile tiles id + 1 Replace the tile with id decos 0 Keep the terrain's decoration, unless the tile is replaced (then the cell has no decoration) decos Deco.Clear (255) Remove the terrain's decoration decos any other id Place that decoration Helpers (tile, deco, fill, border, disc, clearDecos, blit) build layouts. rotated(quarters) and mirrored() return turned and flipped copies. A layout can also carry buildings: footprints drawn as single building sprites that the player can walk into. See buildings-and-interiors.md. World objects A WorldObject (src/world/WorldObject.ts) is a placed layout: id, kind, name, top-left tile position and layout. On creation it lists every chunk its footprint touches. stamp(chunk) writes its part of the layout into a chunk's composed tiles; chests that are opened, or whose anchor was looted before, are drawn as ChestOpen. ObjectManager (src/world/ObjectManager.ts) keeps live objects in a map and indexes them by chunk (every chunk an object covers) and by kind. Placement checks use the indexes: collides looks only at the chunks under the padded rectangle, and crowded only at live objects of the same kind. Multi-chunk lifetime. activeChunks counts how many of an object's chunks are in the cache area. It rises and falls as chunks enter and leave. When it reaches 0, the object is discarded. So a castle covering 3 × 3 chunks survives while any one of those chunks is still loaded, and only goes once all nine have unloaded. Every structure forgets. There are no persistent or kept objects: villages, castles, mines, nomad camps, the extremely rare structures and the rare events are all discarded when their chunks fully unload, like everything else, and may come back different. Nothing about them is saved. What the player did there still counts where it is keyed by the anchor rather than the object: looted chests stay looted and cleared places stay cleared (below). The roll When a chunk enters the cache area, WorldManager.resolveAnchor calls rollAnchor for that chunk. Everything rolled there is \"anchored\" in it. Four independent rolls run, each with its own stream and the region's visit counter: Rare event. For each entry in RARE_EVENTS, in order, roll against its chance; the first hit is placed. Structure. With probability STRUCTURE_CHANCE (0.22): Candidates are structures whose minDistance the chunk's centre has reached and whose biome list (if any) includes the anchor chunk's biome. A tier is picked by weight TIER_WEIGHTS[t] × (1 + TIER_GROWTH[t] × band), with weights 70, 22, 6, 1 and growth 0, 0.3, 1, 2 for common, uncommon, rare and extremely rare. A structure in that tier is picked by its weight. Rift. From RIFT.minDistance (96 tiles) out, a chance by difficulty band (RIFT_CHANCE): 1.4%, 2%, 2.8% and 3.6% per chunk from Easy to Extreme. See Rifts. Lake. Chance by biome: Forest 6% (Lake), Desert 2.5% (Oasis), Snow 5% (Frozen Lake). Placement place() builds the layout with its own Stream.Layout generator, rotates it a random number of quarter turns if the structure allows rotation, and mirrors it half the time. It then picks a random tile in the anchor chunk and positions the layout so it covers that tile and stays within one chunk of the anchor on every side. Structures marked flatGround are dropped unless their footprint is buildable (no mountain, no river) at every point of a grid over it: 3 × 3 points, or 5 × 5 for layouts over 400 tiles. resolveAnchor then rejects a placement if: an object with the same id is already live, it comes within 2 tiles of any live object, or a live object of the same kind has its centre within the structure's spacing. Ids have the form kind@cx,cy#visit/slot. The anchor key cx,cy/slot stays the same across visits. Structure catalogue From STRUCTURES. Rotation \"free\" means any quarter turn; \"mirror\" means flipped only, so building fronts keep facing the camera. Sizes are in tiles. Kind Name Rarity Weight Biomes Min distance Spacing Flat ground Rotation Size camp Camp Common 3 All 0 60 Yes Free 7–9 house Small House Common 3 All 0 40 Yes Mirror 9–11 × 7–8 deposit Ore Deposit Common 2 All 0 30 No Free 6–8 ruins Ruins Uncommon 3 All 0 100 Yes Free 12–18 × 10–15 tower Tower Uncommon 2 All 0 150 Yes Mirror 9–11 × 10–12 cabin Cabin Uncommon 2 Forest, Snow 0 100 Yes Mirror 14–16 × 8–9 abandoned-camp Abandoned Camp Uncommon 2 All 0 80 Yes Free 8–10 nomad-camp Nomad Camp Uncommon 3 Desert 120 260 Yes Mirror 11 mine Mine Uncommon 1 All 0 150 No Mirror 17 × 14 village Village Rare 3 Forest, Snow 150 500 Yes Mirror 46–56 temple Temple Rare 2 All 300 400 Yes Mirror 20–28 × 22–30 castle Castle Rare 2 Forest, Snow 400 800 Yes Mirror 66–76 dungeon Dungeon Rare 2 All 250 300 No Free 12–16 giant-city Giant City Extremely rare 2 All 2,500 3,000 Yes Mirror 84–94 sun-temple Sun Temple Extremely rare 1 Desert 2,500 3,000 Yes Mirror 32–40 boss-arena Boss Arena Extremely rare 2 All 2,500 3,000 Yes Free 37–45 standing-stones Standing Stones Extremely rare 2 All 2,500 3,000 No Free 19–23 Rare events (RARE_EVENTS), rolled per anchor chunk before the structure roll: Kind Name Chance per chunk Rotation ancient-temple Ancient Temple 0.1% Mirror lost-city Lost City 0.01% Mirror crystal-monolith Crystal Monolith 0.001% Free Mines are cut into a ragged rocky hill (dark rock at its heart, lighter at the edges). A timber-framed entrance (a mine building) sits at the foot of the hill, with lamps either side. In front is a dirt yard with rails running out of the entrance to a mine cart, ore heaps, a crate, a barrel and a stone. The entrance leads into a cave; see buildings-and-interiors.md. Nomad camps (nomadCamp(), 11 tiles square) are the desert's travellers, since the desert has no villages: a round patch of path cleared of plants, a campfire in the middle, three tents, an anvil and two barrels. Two people live there: a quest giver and a trader by the fire (living-world.md). Villages (village(), 46–56 tiles square) are laid out along streets: a main street right across, through a cobbled square with the well, an anvil, two benches and four lamps; a cross street north to south; and back lanes 12–13 tiles above and below the main street. Houses (6–8 × 5–6) stand in rows on the north side of each street with 2–4 tiles between them, each with a path from its door to the street, flowers or a barrel by the door, and room cleared around it. Lamps line the main and cross streets. Three to five fenced gardens of tilled soil with rows of vegetables fill open ground. Trees are cleared only on the streets, the square and around houses, so the village sits among trees. Castles (castle()) stand in a moat (shallow at the banks, deep in the middle) crossed by a two-tile drawbridge. Inside it, a curtain wall two tiles thick has round towers at the corners and midway along the east and west walls, and two towers flank the gatehouse. A paved road runs from the gate past lamps to a square before the keep, with a well, a statue and banners. The keep (a keep building, 18–22 × 7) stands at the head of the courtyard, with banners along the north wall and a fenced kitchen garden on each side. Two barracks (stone) face the road with paths to it; below them are a training yard (three dummies, a weapon rack and an armour stand), a smithy (an anvil, a crate and a barrel) and a stable (a cabin). The paved parts are cleared of trees; the rest of the courtyard keeps its own. Building footprints keep the ground they stand on: building() marks the footprint with an invisible solid decoration (Deco.Footprint) instead of a tile, so nothing but the sprite and its own ground ever shows around a building's outline. clearDecos() only clears where the layout has not placed anything, and areaFree() treats footprints as taken. Villages, castles and nomad camps hold an anvil, a crafting station. Camps and nomad camps have a campfire, the other station and a place to rest, beside a canvas tent (abandoned camps keep the tent but not the fire). Villages have lamp posts along their roads that light up at night. Ruins are the shell of an old stone hall: outer walls that stand in runs and collapse in others (switching about every six tiles, with fallen stones spilling inward), a gap in the south wall, an inner wall with a doorway, a colonnade with some pillars fallen to rubble, overgrown patches where the floor has given way, and bushes and flowers creeping in. Chests are always found in castles, dungeons, the Sun Temple, ancient temples and lost cities, and sometimes in villages, temples, towers, ruins, cabins and abandoned camps. Rifts Tears in the air onto an outer dimension, the heart of the story. rift() builds a 7 × 7 layout: decorations cleared, a disc of scorched, violet-veined ground (Tile.RiftGround) and the tear (Deco.Rift, solid) in the middle. The definition is RIFT in StructureGenerator.ts (kind rift, flat ground, spacing 160, mirror only); it is rolled on its own rather than through the tiers, so rifts grow commoner with distance however many other structures there are. A world event can also tear one open near the player (\"A rift tears open nearby.\", see living-world.md). Like every structure a rift is temporary. Its object id (which includes the region's visit) seeds the dimension behind it, so the same rift always leads to the same space. Facing the tear, E enters it (dimensions.md). Closed rifts. Closing a rift calls WorldManager.closeRift: the object is marked closed, so it stamps a faint scar (Deco.RiftScar, not solid) instead of the tear, and its id goes into clearedAnchors, so a re-roll of the same object (its chunks came back before its region regenerated) is closed too. Since the id changes with the region's visit, a regenerated region may open a new rift in the same place. WorldManager.nearestRift finds the nearest open rift among live objects (through ObjectManager.ofKind) for the story's arrow and the glitches around it. Lakes lake() builds a natural-looking lake rather than an ellipse: Two to four overlapping elliptical lobes, with positions and radii scaled to a rolled size of 18 to 58 tiles. The sample position is warped by noise (±4 tiles) and a little fractal noise is added, so the edge has bays and points. Two smoothing passes remove single-tile notches; water patches under 20 tiles and land patches under 6 tiles (islands not touching the edge) are flipped. A two-pass chamfer distance transform measures distance from the shore, inward and outward. Water more than about 3 tiles from shore (wobbling with noise) is deep. The beach is patchy: mostly one ring of shore tiles, with a second ring where noise allows. Desert lakes (oases) get palms on the outer beach ring. Snow lakes are ice with a few open-water holes in the deep middle. The layout is cropped to its contents so its collision box stays tight. Chests, cleared places and harvesting Chests never refill. Opening a chest marks it opened on its object and adds the object's anchor key to WorldManager.lootedAnchors. If the region later regenerates and a new structure rolls at the same anchor, every chest in it starts open and empty. Chest loot is seeded by the object id and chest position. Cleared places stay cleared. Dungeons, boss arenas and legendary creatures record a key in WorldManager.clearedAnchors when their guards fall, and never wake again. Closed rifts are recorded by object id instead, so they stay closed only while their region stands (above). Harvest log. Gathering a decoration records the cell, its decoration and the in-game minute, per chunk, tagged with the region's visit counter. Composition removes those decorations while the record's visit matches. When the region regenerates, the record no longer applies and is deleted. Bushes and flowers grow back after 1,440 in-game minutes and mushrooms after 2,160 (REGROW_MINUTES), checked once per in-game hour by WorldManager.regrow(). Other placed objects Some objects are not rolled with the terrain: world events (src/systems/WorldEvents.ts) place a meteor crater, buried treasure, a risen obelisk, a rift or a Moonwell on open, loaded ground near the player. They go through WorldManager.addObject, so they stamp, stream and are forgotten like any other object. See also: World generation, World streaming, Saves "},{"slug":"rendering","title":"Rendering","group":"Engine","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"pixel-scaling","text":"Pixel scaling"},{"level":2,"id":"scene","text":"Scene"},{"level":2,"id":"the-atlas","text":"The atlas"},{"level":2,"id":"chunk-meshes","text":"Chunk meshes"},{"level":2,"id":"rifts-and-their-glitches","text":"Rifts and their glitches"},{"level":2,"id":"outer-dimensions","text":"Outer dimensions"},{"level":2,"id":"buildings-and-interiors","text":"Buildings and interiors"},{"level":2,"id":"the-player","text":"The player"},{"level":2,"id":"entities","text":"Entities"},{"level":2,"id":"lighting","text":"Lighting"},{"level":2,"id":"weather","text":"Weather"},{"level":2,"id":"effects","text":"Effects"},{"level":2,"id":"html-ui","text":"HTML UI"},{"level":2,"id":"low-quality-settings","text":"Low-quality settings"}],"text":"Rendering How the world is drawn: a procedural atlas, one mesh per chunk, y-sorted sprites, night lighting, weather and an HTML UI. Pixel scaling The canvas renders at a low internal resolution and is scaled up with nearest-neighbour filtering (Game.resize in src/game/Game.ts): Scale = max(1, floor(window height / TARGET_HEIGHT)), with TARGET_HEIGHT = 270. A 1080-pixel window renders at 480 × 270 internally, scaled ×4. The renderer resolution is 1 (device pixel ratio is ignored), roundPixels is on, and TextureStyle.defaultOptions.scaleMode is 'nearest'. The CSS sets image-rendering: pixelated on the canvas. The camera zoom (0.25 to 2, steps of ×0.8, snapping to exactly 1 near 1) scales the world layer, and the render area grows or shrinks to match. The frame rate is capped at 60 (app.ticker.maxFPS), or 30 with the setting, so 120 Hz screens do not do twice the work. Scene Render groups. The ground (chunk meshes) and the y-sorted entities are separate Pixi render groups (isRenderGroup), so the entities re-sorting every frame never makes Pixi rebuild the ground's draw list, and the other way round. No Pixi pointer events. Input is plain DOM events (src/game/Input.ts), so Pixi's event system is off: eventFeatures are all false and the stage's eventMode is 'none'. Pixi would otherwise hit-test the whole scene on every pointer move. The atlas Every image is painted in code at startup by buildAtlas() (src/rendering/Atlas.ts) into one 256-pixel-wide canvas. No image files ship. Region Contents Layout Tiles 41 tile types × 4 variants 16 × 16 cells, cell = tileId × 4 + variant Decorations 36 decoration types Shelf-packed frames from DECO_Y; most are 16 × 32, plants and trees larger (oaks and palms 32 × 48, pines 24 × 48, cacti 24 × 40, crystals 24 × 32, bushes 24 × 24, flowers, mushrooms and dead bushes 16 × 16, the Anchor core 32 × 48) Humans 8 outfits × 4 directions × 4 walk frames 16 × 32 cells from PLAYER_Y, one row per outfit Items One icon per item 16 × 16 cells from ITEM_Y Creatures and projectiles Two walk frames per species with its own art, the buried sand lurker, and four projectiles Shelf-packed frames from CREATURE_Y Armour 3 sets (ARMOR_SETS: leather, iron, starforged) × parts × 16 frames One row of 16 frames per set and part, from ARMOR_Y The armour region starts at ARMOR_Y, below the creatures, with 32-pixel rows. ATLAS_H is its bottom. Painters. A small Painter class draws into an ImageData: pixels with alpha blending, rectangles, ellipses, speckles, soft shadows, and outline(), which adds a dark one-pixel outline around opaque pixels for the Stardew look. Ground tiles use per-palette colour sets and speckle styles (grass, sand, snow, dirt). Foliage uses foliage(), which shades a leafy cluster from the top left with noise and ragged edges, then adds random leaf flecks. Outfits. OUTFITS holds skin, hair, shirt and trouser colours, plus an optional apron, robe, mask or rags: player, villager-a, villager-b, villager-c, trader, merchant, lost and bandit. playerCell(dir, frame, outfit) returns a frame. Lookup helpers: tileCell, decoFrame, itemCell, playerCell, creatureFrame (keys such as wolf:1, lurker:buried or projectile:arrow), and for the outer dimensions dimCell (16 × 16 tiles from DIM_Y: void, floor, wall tops and faces, crumbling and glitching slabs, plates, safe pads, runes) and dimFrame (props: pillars, crystals, gates, the vault chest, the portal, the rift, mural stones). Rifts in the overworld. Deco.Rift (32 × 48) is frame 0 of a jagged violet and cyan tear over the scorched Tile.RiftGround; frames 1 and 2 (tear:1, tear:2 among the dimension props) are the same tear with its seam wobbled and its glitch slices moved. riftFrame(f) returns frame f of RIFT_FRAMES (3). A closed rift shows Deco.RiftScar (16 × 16). Chunk meshes TileRenderer (src/rendering/TileRenderer.ts) implements ChunkListener: Each shown chunk is one Mesh of 1,024 quads over the atlas texture, so a chunk is one draw call. All meshes share the same positions and indices; only UVs change. The tile variant is hash3(gx, gy, 17) % 4, so ground tiles do not repeat visibly. Stone and wood walls draw their top or their front face depending on the tile below; see buildings-and-interiors.md. UVs are pulled in by 0.02 pixels (INSET) so neighbouring atlas cells never bleed when zoomed out. Meshes and decoration sprites are pooled and reused when chunks are hidden. Culling. Chunks are shown a chunk beyond the screen (the render area), but cull() hides those outside the camera's view, padded by 6 tiles (CULL_PAD) for tall decorations and light glow. A culled chunk's mesh is hidden and its decoration sprites are taken out of the sorted entities container, so they cost nothing to sort. Game calls cull every frame with the camera's view. Drawn a few at a time. Drawing a chunk is costly (a mesh, hundreds of sprites, lights), so ChunkManager queues ready chunks (pendingShow) and shows at most 2 per frame (SHOW_PER_FRAME), nearest to the player first. Without a renderer (tests) chunks show at once. Decorations are sprites anchored at their bottom centre, positioned on their tile and added to the shared entities container with zIndex = their foot position, so they sort with the player, NPCs and enemies. Tree fading. fadeAround(px, py) checks sprites in the 3 × 3 chunks around the player. Any sprite at least 24 pixels tall whose foot is below the player's and whose box overlaps the player's body is drawn at 45% opacity, so the player is never lost behind a tree. Lights. While collecting a chunk's sprites, collectLights also records light sources in world pixels: campfires (radius 5 tiles, warm), fireplaces, lamps, meteorites, Moonwells, and windowed facade tiles (radius 1.8 tiles). lights(out) returns them for chunks in view only. Rifts and their glitches TileRenderer keeps the rift sprites of drawn chunks in a list as decorations are added and released (usually empty) and animate(dt) swaps their texture between the three frames at 7 frames a second: textures swapped, never redrawn, and nothing at all without a rift. Near an open rift the world glitches (src/rendering/RiftGlitch.ts). The game finds the nearest open rift four times a second, with the HUD; only while one is within 12 tiles, and the game is not paused, does RiftGlitch.update run each frame. Every 0.7 to 4.5 seconds (sooner nearer the rift) one of: Jolt: one to three decorations near the rift jump 2 to 5 pixels sideways, tinted violet, cyan or pink, for 0.08 to 0.2 s (TileRenderer.jolt, put back by settle; a chunk released or redrawn meanwhile settles first, so pooled sprites never keep a jolt). Colour slip: the world layer is tinted and torn 2 pixels sideways for 0.06 to 0.14 s. HUD stutter: the HUD's panels jump and shift hue for 0.18 s (Hud.stutter, a CSS animation). Colour slips and HUD stutters follow the \"Screen flashes\" setting, and reduced motion stops the stutter. Out of range everything is put back once and nothing runs. The rift's hum is in audio.md. Outer dimensions DimensionRenderer (src/rendering/DimensionRenderer.ts) draws a dimension into the overworld's own ground and entities layers, while the overworld's sprites are hidden: the chunk meshes culled to nowhere (TileRenderer.hideAll), buildings and ground items cleared, NPCs not passed to the entity renderer. Its static floor is one batched mesh per 16 × 16 block over the atlas (as chunks are); tiles that change (crumbling and glitching slabs, plates) are flat overlay sprites; props (pillars, crystals, mural stones, gates, the chest, the portal, the rift) are y-sorted among the player and enemies. Everything is built in open(); frames only cull blocks to the camera and swap textures of slots in view whose look changed. Meshes, sprites and lights are pooled across trips. Crystals, the rift, safe pads and the portal light the dark in view only. See dimensions.md. Buildings and interiors Buildings are painted once per building into cached canvas textures and drawn as y-sorted sprites (src/rendering/Buildings.ts); their windows are night lights. Which buildings to show is rescanned only when the render area changes, or every 30 frames (RESCAN_FRAMES); in between, only the fade of a building the player walks behind is updated. Interiors have their own view (src/rendering/InteriorRenderer.ts): a sprite per tile, furniture and residents y-sorted with the player, and fireplace and lamp lights. Details are in buildings-and-interiors.md. The player The player (src/game/Player.ts) is a container: the body sprite, the item in hand, and a two-pixel fist drawn over the grip. The hand position follows the arms in the human sprite (handAt), including the walk sway. Holding: the selected hotbar item is shown if it is a weapon, tool or light. Weapons, tools and torches are carried upright, tipped away from the body; lanterns hang from their handle and sway while walking; a bow is held at the side. Facing away, the item is drawn behind the body. Nothing is held while swimming or rolling. Actions (act()): a swing (0.28 s) winds the item up behind and sweeps it through the facing, with a 2-pixel lunge; a punch (0.2 s) is a lunge with empty hands; a shot (0.3 s) holds the bow out in front; a pick (0.4 s) is a stoop. Attacks play a swing, shot or punch; chopping and mining swing the best axe or pickaxe carried (or punch bare-handed) on every strike; gathering plants stoops. Armour and off hand: worn armour is drawn as overlays (armorCell() in the atlas: one row of 16 frames per set and part) over the body, legs and feet first, then chest and head, matching every direction and walk frame. The off-hand item sits in the other hand, or in front of the body when the shield is raised. layer() orders everything back to front each frame: what is held on the far side, the body and its armour, then what is held on the near side. Swimming: only the top 24 pixels of the body show, sunk 4 pixels and bobbing; leg and foot armour are hidden. Entities EntityRenderer (src/rendering/EntityRenderer.ts) draws what moves: Enemies and animals. Creature frames with two walk frames, mirrored by facing (rabbits, hares, deer, boars, lizards and goats included). Humanoid species (bandits) use the four-direction human sprites instead. Variants draw another species' frames (art), tinted (tint) and scaled (size), such as the pale Frost Wolf or the large dark-red Dune Tyrant. Elites are drawn 1.25 times larger and tinted gold. A hit flashes red; the attack wind-up flickers pink. A health bar shows once an enemy is hurt (bosses use the HUD bar instead); it is drawn around its own origin and moved, and redrawn only when the shown health changes. A yellow \"!\" marks enemies that have just noticed the player, and a red \"!\" one winding up a blow. NPCs. Human sprites by outfit, with the walk cycle while moving, hidden while indoors, scaled (children are smaller) and translucent if they are a ghost. A floating symbol marks them: \"!\" for news or work, \"$\" for trade, \"?\" for the lost, \"✓\" for a quest hand-in. Projectiles. Pooled sprites; arrows and ice shards point along their flight, webs and boulders spin. Damage numbers. Text that floats up and fades over 0.8 seconds: white, yellow for critical hits, red for damage to the player. Swings. A white arc in front of the player for 0.14 seconds. Pooling. Enemy sprites with their bars and marks, damage numbers (by style) and swing arcs are hidden and kept for reuse instead of destroyed, since making a Text costs a texture upload. Lighting Lighting (src/rendering/Lighting.ts) adds darkness without a custom shader: A RenderTexture the size of the internal canvas is cleared and filled with a dark colour: deep blue (0x080c22) when darkness is above 0.6, purple dusk (0x2a1430) otherwise, at darkness × 0.8 opacity. The fill is a tinted white sprite, cheaper to change each frame than a Graphics. For each light, a soft radial gradient sprite is drawn with the erase blend mode, cutting a hole in the darkness. Warm lights flicker slightly. The texture is shown above the world. A second set of the same gradient sprites, tinted orange with the add blend mode at warmth × 0.35 × darkness, gives fires and windows a warm glow. Darkness comes from the clock, plus up to 0.45 during a thunderstorm, plus a faint gloom of 0.03 per difficulty band. Inside an outer dimension it is the dimension's own (mood.darkness, 0.55 for The Hollow) at any hour, in its own colour (update(..., tint), violet for The Hollow), with no weather. The player carries a light: 2.4 tiles with nothing, 5 with a torch and 7 with a lantern. Burning trees are lights too. Flat tint. When lights would not matter (by day, where only the band's gloom darkens the screen) or the Lighting setting is off, update(..., flat) shows a single tinted sprite over the screen instead (at 85% of the strength), with no light holes and no render pass. Lights are not even gathered then. Weather ParticleSystem (src/rendering/ParticleSystem.ts) draws weather in screen space, above the lighting: Weather Particles Veil Rain 140 rain streaks Grey-blue Thunderstorm 220 faster, slanted streaks Dark blue Snow 110 slow flakes White Blizzard 240 fast sideways flakes White Sandstorm 180 sand streaks blowing in from the right Tan Fog None Pale grey Heat wave None Faint yellow Particle counts scale with the screen area (relative to 480 × 270) and fade in and out as the weather changes. Particles no longer needed are hidden and kept for later. Lightning draws a white flash that fades over 0.25 seconds, unless the \"Screen flashes\" setting is off. The veil and the flash are tinted white sprites. Effects Effects (src/rendering/Effects.ts) draws world-space effects. Fires, lightning warnings and meteors are rare, so they share one Graphics, cleared only when something was or is to be drawn. Particles and fireflies are pooled tinted square sprites, cheap to move every frame. Fires. Flickering flame circles and rising smoke over burning trees. Lightning warnings. A circle that fills in over the 1.2 seconds before a strike lands. Meteors. A streak falling toward the impact point, then an expanding shock ring. Particle bursts. burst(x, y, kind) throws small squares with their own speed, gravity and lifetime: wood and stone chips while gathering, leaf for plants, dust for running, dodging and defeated enemies, hit sparks on damage, magic for crystals, gold for chests and level-ups. At most 400 live at once. The \"Hit and dust particles\" setting turns bursts off. Ambient life. Fireflies drift around the player on calm forest nights and add small glowing lights to the lighting layer. Falling leaves drift and sway in autumn forests by day. Both follow the weather particles setting. Ground items (src/rendering/GroundItemRenderer.ts) are drawn only within 2 tiles of the screen, and every item shares one shadow shape (GraphicsContext). HTML UI The interface is HTML and CSS over the canvas (src/ui/, styled in src/style.css), so it costs nothing while closed: Component Shows Hud Compass, location panel (clock, weather, biome, difficulty, position), quest tracker with direction arrows, notifications, tips, alerts, action prompt, vitals, level and XP, status effects, boss bar, hurt flash, controls bar (naming the player's keys), touch buttons, loading screen Hotbar The first 8 inventory slots, with counts and weapon durability bars; the selected item's name shows above the bar for 1.6 s Menu Tabs: Inventory (with Sort bag), Crafting (with ×N), Skills, Quests (with Abandon and the legends), Map (hidden where maps don't work), Settings (with key bindings on desktop), About (privacy, credits, licenses; About.ts) MapView The overworld map canvas, kept for if maps ever work there; see exploration.md DimensionMap The map inside an outer dimension: seen tiles, chambers, the rift, the portal, the player; see dimensions.md PauseMenu Esc or the touch Pause button: Resume, Save, Settings, Full screen (where supported), New world, and the controls table (touch controls on touch screens, else the player's keys) Dialog NPC conversation and trade Vision Full-screen story text, one page at a time, with queued visions DebugOverlay F3: seed, frame rate, chunk counts, biomes, objects, enemies, regions, and a minimap of the cache area TouchStick The virtual stick on touch screens Hints First-time tips (Hints.ts); see below Intro The opening screen (Intro.ts, markup in index.html): the title over darkness from the first paint; once the player is placed, the darkness breaks into 40px squares that clear outward from the centre in a ragged ring (about 1.3 s), then it is removed. A low-resolution canvas (8 pixels per square) scaled up with image-rendering: pixelated; reduced motion gets a 0.4 s fade. Later trips (death, travel) use the HUD's loading screen. Fullscreen Full screen helpers for the Full button and the pause menu (Fullscreen.ts) SleepOverlay, Playground The sleep transition; the story playground panel (development builds), with the rift tools Item icons are copied from the atlas canvas into small canvases (ItemIcon.ts). Panels use a wood-framed style and the Pixelify Sans font. Messages. notify shows a short note bottom left. tip shows a \"Tip:\" note that stays 9 seconds. alert shows a message in the middle of the screen that stays until the player presses OK; the same text is never shown twice at once. Alerts carry what must not be missed: a collapse, a save that could not be read, another tab holding the save, a learned recipe. First-time tips (src/ui/Hints.ts) show once per browser, one at a time, the first time each applies: start (how to move and interact), an enemy hunting the player, hungry, thirsty, night, near a campfire, near an anvil, a skill point to spend, a heavy bag, near a rift (how to enter, and what dying there costs), and inside a dimension (the map works there). Shown ids are kept in localStorage under untrue.hints (once per session if storage is blocked). The story playground shows none. Tips name the player's own keys, or the touch buttons on touch screens. Cheap updates. The HUD refreshes several times a second but writes to the page only when a value changed (Hud.changed). The vitals, XP and boss bars move with transform: scale instead of resizing. The touch stick caches its resting spot and moves by transform. Under the system's reduced-motion setting, all HUD and menu animation stops. Text and HUD size. The setting (0.8 to 1.5) sets --ui-scale, which zooms #ui with CSS zoom. Low-quality settings The Settings tab (stored in localStorage) can switch off: Setting Effect when off Lighting No lights: night is a plain tint (see Lighting) Weather particles The weather layer (particles and veil) is hidden Hit and dust particles No particle bursts Cap at 30 frames per second Sets the ticker's maxFPS to 30 when on (60 when off) Screen flashes No lightning flash, no red hurt flash, and no colour slips or HUD stutters near a rift; off by default under reduced motion Show the controls bar Hides the controls bar The same tab has music and sound effects volume sliders; see audio.md. See also: Architecture, World streaming "},{"slug":"saves","title":"Saves","group":"Engine","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"what-is-saved","text":"What is saved"},{"level":2,"id":"format","text":"Format"},{"level":2,"id":"versions","text":"Versions"},{"level":2,"id":"saves-that-cannot-be-read","text":"Saves that cannot be read"},{"level":2,"id":"storage","text":"Storage"},{"level":2,"id":"two-tabs","text":"Two tabs"},{"level":2,"id":"settings-and-tips","text":"Settings and tips"}],"text":"Saves What a save holds, where it lives, and how older saves are upgraded. What is saved Saves stay small because the world itself is never stored: terrain, biomes, every structure, decorations and enemies all regenerate from the seed. Saved Not saved World seed Terrain and decorations Player position, inventory (with weapon durability), worn equipment, vitals, level, XP and skill ranks Biome records Where the player wakes after collapsing (respawn) and the Moonwells already drunk from World objects: every structure, rare or not (they regenerate) Legends defeated (trophies) and rare recipes learned (recipes), inside progression Anchors whose chests were looted (lootedAnchors) Enemies, projectiles, NPCs and fires Dungeons, arenas and legendary spots that were cleared, and closed rifts by object id (clearedAnchors) A trip into an outer dimension: its run, enemies and map Region visit counters, so regions come back as the player left them The harvest log, so gathered plants stay gathered until they regrow Clock, per-biome weather and quests The rift story: rifts closed, and whether the player has entered one Explored chunks and map markers Lost travellers from rescue quests are not saved as NPCs; their position lives in the quest record and they reappear when their spot is simulated. Format SaveData (src/save/SaveManager.ts): interface SaveData { version: number; // SAVE_VERSION, currently 8 seed: number; savedAt: number; // Date.now() player: { x: number; y: number; inventory: SavedSlot[]; // { id, count, durability? } or null, 24 slots equipment?: Partial<Record<EquipSlot, SavedSlot>>; // head, chest, legs, feet, offhand vitals: VitalValues | null; // health, stamina, hunger, thirst progression: ProgressionSave | null; // xp, level, skills?, trophies?, recipes? respawn?: { x, y }; // where the player wakes after collapsing moonwells?: string[]; // ids of Moonwells already drunk from }; lootedAnchors: string[]; clearedAnchors: string[]; living: { // null in saves from before the living world time: { day, minutes }; weather: Record<biome, { kind, hoursLeft }>; quests: Quest[]; rift?: { version: 1, closed: number, entered: boolean }; // the story; missing before it } | null; exploration: { // null before the map existed explored: number[]; // flat pairs: chunk key, biome palette + 1 markers: { id, x, y, label }[]; tracked: number | null; } | null; regions: number[]; // flat pairs: region key, visit counter (only counters above 0) harvested: { key, visit, cells: number[] }[]; // per chunk: flat triples of cell, decoration, minute ground: { id, count, durability?, x, y }[]; // items dropped on the ground } snapshot(world, player, living, exploration) builds it; restore(world, data) puts the looted and cleared anchors, region visits, harvest log and ground items back into a fresh world built from the same seed. Game restores inventory, equipment, progression, vitals, the waking spot and the used Moonwells, and LivingWorld.load restores the clock, weather, quests and the rift story. Fields marked ? are missing in older saves and load with defaults: the best armour carried is put on, there are no skills, trophies or recipes, the player wakes at the start, no Moonwell has been used, and the story starts at \"Find a rift\" with no rifts closed (and without the opening vision, which only a new world shows). The rift story is an optional field, so it needed no new save version. Saving in a dimension. As indoors, the save keeps the player's spot in the overworld: the doorstep outside the rift (Place.spot). Nothing of the trip is saved, so a reload starts outside the rift with the run's progress gone. A rift from a world event is not saved either, so after a reload it may not be there. Versions migrate() upgrades older saves one step at a time: From Change 1 Adds an empty inventory, vitals: null, progression: null, anchor: '' and opened: [] on objects, and lootedAnchors: []. 2 Adds progression: null and clearedAnchors: []. 3 Adds living: null. 4 Adds exploration: null. 5 Adds buildings: [] to every object, regions: [] and harvested: []. 6 Adds ground: []. 7 Every structure now forgets and the old story is gone: drops objects (persistent places, story places and kept villages, castles, mines and nomad camps), known (the register of discovered places) and living.story (the stage, flags, ending, whose world rules go with it, and the lantern memories). The Wanderer's Lantern, in the bag, the hotbar or the off hand, becomes an ordinary Lantern, so the player keeps a light that never burns out. The version 1 and 5 steps still fill in fields on an old save's objects; the version 7 step then drops them. Saves that cannot be read The game never lets a new world overwrite a save it could not use (SaveManager.load(), Game.start()): Problem What happens The stored save has an unknown or newer version, or is malformed It is copied to a backup key (backup-<time>) in the same store and UnreadableSave is thrown. The game starts a new world and shows an alert saying the old save was kept. Storage fails to read at all A save may exist, so this session does not save (saveLock). An alert says to reload. The living-world part (quests, weather, clock) fails to load The rest of the save loads and the living world starts afresh. The old save is backed up and an alert says so. Alerts (Hud.alert) stay on screen until the player presses OK. Storage IndexedDB database untrue, object store saves, one slot with key main. Autosave every 30 seconds, and whenever the page is hidden (tab switch or minimise). Ctrl+S saves now and confirms with a notification. Nothing is saved while the player is travelling (waiting to be placed after a jump or a collapse), or while a vision is open, since a story may be half way through a change. A write counts as done once its IndexedDB transaction completes, not when the request succeeds. ?new or ?seed=N starts a fresh world; its first autosave replaces the slot. Two tabs Two tabs would save over the same slot. The first tab takes a Web Lock (navigator.locks, lock untrue-save) and holds it until it closes. A later tab cannot get the lock, so it plays without saving and shows an alert. Where Web Locks are missing, a BroadcastChannel named untrue does the same: a new tab says hello, and if an open tab answers, the new tab stops saving. The story playground never saves, so it does not take the lock. Settings and tips Settings are separate: they live in localStorage under untrue.settings (lighting, weather, particles, fps30, flashes, help, music, sfx, uiScale, keys), per browser, and fall back to defaults if storage is blocked. First-time tips already shown are kept under untrue.hints (src/ui/Hints.ts). The pause menu's \"New world\" clears the save slot and reloads with ?new. See also: Objects and structures, Architecture "},{"slug":"survival","title":"Survival","group":"Gameplay","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"vitals","text":"Vitals"},{"level":3,"id":"drain","text":"Drain"},{"level":3,"id":"health","text":"Health"},{"level":3,"id":"low-warnings","text":"Low warnings"},{"level":3,"id":"status-effects","text":"Status effects"},{"level":3,"id":"collapse","text":"Collapse"},{"level":2,"id":"stamina","text":"Stamina"},{"level":2,"id":"gathering","text":"Gathering"},{"level":3,"id":"drinking","text":"Drinking"},{"level":2,"id":"swimming","text":"Swimming"},{"level":2,"id":"items","text":"Items"},{"level":2,"id":"inventory-and-hotbar","text":"Inventory and hotbar"},{"level":3,"id":"weight","text":"Weight"},{"level":3,"id":"dropping-and-picking-up","text":"Dropping and picking up"},{"level":3,"id":"equipment","text":"Equipment"},{"level":2,"id":"crafting","text":"Crafting"},{"level":3,"id":"rare-recipes","text":"Rare recipes"},{"level":2,"id":"loot","text":"Loot"},{"level":3,"id":"chests","text":"Chests"},{"level":3,"id":"barrels","text":"Barrels"},{"level":3,"id":"buried-treasure","text":"Buried treasure"},{"level":3,"id":"enemy-drops","text":"Enemy drops"},{"level":2,"id":"light","text":"Light"}],"text":"Survival How the player stays alive: vitals, stamina, gathering, items, the inventory, crafting, loot and light. Vitals src/systems/Vitals.ts keeps four bars: health, stamina, hunger and thirst. Hunger, thirst and stamina run from 0 to 100. Health runs from 0 to maxHealth, which starts at 100 and grows with level and the Toughness skill (see combat.md). Drain Bar Full drain at rest Multipliers Hunger 600 s ×1.5 while running, biome, weather Thirst 420 s ×1.5 while running, biome, weather Biome multipliers (BIOME_HUNGER, BIOME_THIRST): Biome Hunger Thirst Forest 1 1 Desert 1 1.6 Snow 1.4 0.9 Weather multiplies these again; the table is in living-world.md. Health Health changes once per frame from the sum of these rates: Condition Effect Hunger or thirst at 0 −1.2 HP/s for each empty bar Hunger and thirst both above 40, not poisoned +0.6 HP/s Poisoned −2.5 HP/s Starvation and healing never apply at the same time: any empty bar stops healing. Low warnings The HUD (src/ui/Hud.ts) warns when a bar runs low. Each bar is low or critical below a share of its maximum, and clears 3% above it, so warnings do not flicker: Bar Low Critical Health 30% (\"Low health\") 12% (\"Near death!\") Hunger 20% (\"Hungry\") empty (\"Starving!\") Thirst 20% (\"Thirsty\") empty (\"Dehydrated!\") Stamina – 12% (\"Exhausted\") A banner at the top centre lists the warnings, worst first; critical ones are red and blink. The bar's frame glows and pulses, its label turns red, and a critical bar shakes. Low health tints the screen edges red; critical health makes them throb like a heartbeat. Each time a bar gets worse an alarm sounds (warn; not for stamina, which runs out often). Animations stop under the system's reduced-motion setting. Status effects Status Effect Source Cure poison −2.5 HP/s, stops healing Hits from scorpions, Emperor Scorpions, Thorn Spiders (their webs), Ghouls and the Dune Tyrant Antidote, a Moonwell, or wait it out slow Movement speed ×0.55 (SLOW_FACTOR) Spider webs, Frost Golem ice Wait it out Each status lasts a set number of seconds. A new hit refreshes it to whichever is longer. Collapse When health reaches 0 the player collapses. Game.collapse() leaves any building, cancels gathering, calls Vitals.revive() and sends the player to their waking spot, with an alert that stays until dismissed. The waking spot (Game.respawn) is the start (0, 0) until the player: rests at a campfire or in a bed (the spot outside the door, if indoors), or drinks from a Moonwell. Each of these moves it to where the player stands (Game.setRespawn). It is saved as player.respawn (saves.md). Revival sets: health to 60% of maxHealth stamina to 100 hunger and thirst to at least 50 poison and slow to 0 The player keeps everything they carry, except behind a rift: dying in an outer dimension loses everything in the bag and the hotbar (worn equipment stays), and the rift throws the player back out at its doorstep instead of the waking spot (dimensions.md). Stamina Use Cost Notes Running 18/s Needs stamina above 1. Blocking also stops running. Dodge roll 20, 12% less per Nimble rank Needs the full cost. Blocking a hit 8 per blocked hit No stamina means the block fails. Gathering 4 per completed gather Below 4 stamina, gathering takes twice as long. Stamina recovers at 22/s while not running, or at 30% of that rate while hunger or thirst is empty. Movement speed is 5 tiles/s walking and 9 running (WALK_SPEED, RUN_SPEED in src/config.ts). Gathering Press E facing a decoration, or standing on one. src/systems/GatheringSystem.ts checks the tile in front first, then the tile under the feet. Gathering runs while the player stands still; moving, taking a hit, attacking or dodging cancels it. Time is seconds ÷ (1 + tool tier), doubled when stamina is below 4. Tools work automatically: the highest tier of the right kind anywhere in the bag counts. Decoration Base time Tool Min tier Drops Oak, autumn oak, pine, snowy pine, palm 4 s axe 0 Wood ×(1–2 + tier) Dead bush 0.7 s – 0 Wood ×1 Bush 0.7 s – 0 Berries ×1–3, 30% chance of 1 Herb Flowers 0.7 s – 0 Herb ×1–2 Mushroom 0.7 s – 0 Mushroom ×1 Garden vegetables (villages) 0.7 s – 0 Vegetables ×1–2, Herb (20%) Cactus 1.2 s – 0 Cactus Fruit ×1–2 Stone, red rock, snowy rock 3.5 s pickaxe 0 Stone ×(1–2 + tier) Ore 4 s pickaxe 1 1–2 of: Gold Ore (12% + 5% per band, with tier 2 or more), Coal (the next 40%, plus the gold share below tier 2), else Iron Ore Crystal 5 s pickaxe 2 Crystal ×1 (×2 with a 15% chance per band); a Gemstone too, 6% + 3% per band Barrel 0.8 s – 0 Barrel loot (below) Buried treasure (X mark) 1.6 s – 0 Coin ×10–40 plus a chest roll at band 2 Meteorite 5 s pickaxe 1 Star Metal ×(2–3 + tier) Below the minimum tier the prompt reads \"Needs a pickaxe\" or \"Needs an iron pickaxe\". Tiers run from stone (1) through iron (2) to starforged (3). Rare finds get likelier further out: the band is the difficulty band where the rock stands. In a mine it is the band of the mine's door outside (GatheringSystem.bandAt). Drops are seeded by the tile and the region's visit counter, so the same plant gives the same drop until its region regenerates. Gathered decorations are removed through WorldManager.harvest(). They stay gone until the region regenerates, except plants that regrow (living-world.md). If the bag is full, what does not fit is left on the ground at the player's feet (outside the door, if indoors) and a notice says so. The same goes for enemy drops (where the enemy fell), quest rewards and the village defence reward. Drinking Facing water with nothing on it, E drinks: Tile Thirst Water, deep water +25 Ice +12 Swimming Rivers and lakes can be swum (Game.walker lets the player through water tiles with nothing on them; enemies, villagers and the world's own collision still treat water as solid). Water Speed Stamina Shallow (Water) 55% Recovers as normal Deep (WaterDeep) 42% Drains as if running (18/s), and hunger and thirst drain 1.5× No running, dodging or blocking while swimming. Out of stamina in deep water, the player starts to drown: 4 health a second, with a warning every 4 seconds, until they reach shallow water or land. Only the head and shoulders show, bobbing, with splashes while moving. Arrows and other shots fly over water. Bridges cross rivers every so often; see world-generation.md. Items src/items/Items.ts. The default stack is 99. Food effects apply through Vitals.consume() when used. Item Rarity Stack Use Wood Common 99 Crafting Stone Common 99 Crafting Coal Common 99 Iron ingots, torches Iron Ore Uncommon 99 Iron ingots Gold Ore Rare 99 Star ingots; sell (12c) Crystal Epic 99 Crystal Bow, Frost and Spirit Blades; sell (35c) Herb Common 99 Bandages, stew, antidote, Health Potion, Frost Draught Berries Common 99 Food: +12 hunger, +4 thirst Mushroom Common 99 Food: +10 hunger Vegetables Common 99 Food: +14 hunger, +4 thirst; sells for 2c Cactus Fruit Common 99 Food: +6 hunger, +20 thirst Roast Mushroom Uncommon 99 Food: +28 hunger Forest Stew Uncommon 99 Food: +45 hunger, +15 thirst Herbal Bandage Uncommon 99 +25 health Health Potion Rare 99 +60 health Frost Draught Uncommon 99 +45 thirst, +10 health Iron Ingot Uncommon 99 Iron tools, weapons, armor, lantern Star Ingot Epic 99 Starforged tools, weapons and armour; sell (120c) Stone Axe Common 1 Axe tier 1 Stone Pickaxe Common 1 Pickaxe tier 1 Iron Axe Uncommon 1 Axe tier 2 Iron Pickaxe Rare 1 Pickaxe tier 2 Starforged Axe Epic 1 Axe tier 3 Starforged Pickaxe Epic 1 Pickaxe tier 3 Gemstone Epic 99 Starforged Sword; sell (60c) Golden Idol Legendary 99 Sell (250c) Wooden Sword Common 1 Weapon (see combat.md) Iron Sword Rare 1 Weapon Frost Blade, Venom Blade, Spirit Blade Epic 1 Weapons with a damage type Starforged Sword Legendary 1 Weapon Hunting Bow Uncommon 1 Weapon, uses arrows Crystal Bow Epic 1 Weapon, uses arrows Arrow Common 99 Bow ammunition Leather Cap Common 1 Head armour, 4% Leather Tunic Uncommon 1 Chest armour, 8% Leather Trousers Uncommon 1 Leg armour, 5% Leather Boots Common 1 Foot armour, 3% Iron Helmet Rare 1 Head armour, 8% Iron Chestplate Epic 1 Chest armour, 16% Iron Greaves Rare 1 Leg armour, 10% Iron Boots Rare 1 Foot armour, 6% Starforged Helm Epic 1 Head armour, 11% Starforged Plate Legendary 1 Chest armour, 22% Starforged Greaves Epic 1 Leg armour, 14% Starforged Boots Epic 1 Foot armour, 9% Wooden Shield Common 1 Off hand: blocks 85% of a blow for 6 stamina Chitin Shield Uncommon 1 Off hand: blocks 90% of a blow for 5 stamina Iron Shield Rare 1 Off hand: blocks 95% of a blow for 4 stamina Raw Meat Common 99 Food: +8 hunger Cooked Meat Uncommon 99 Food: +35 hunger, +5 health Hide Common 99 Leather, iron and starforged armour Spider Silk Common 99 Bows Chitin Common 99 Chitin Shield, Starforged Plate; sell (3c) Venom Sac Uncommon 99 Venom Blade; sell (6c) Ice Shard Uncommon 99 Frost Draught, Frost Blade; sell (6c) Bone Common 99 Arrows; sell (1c) Antidote Uncommon 99 +5 health, cures poison Coin Common 999 Currency Torch Common 99 Light, radius 5 Lantern Rare 1 Light, radius 7, never burns out Spirit Essence Uncommon 99 Lantern, Health Potion, Spirit Blade; sell (8c) Star Metal Epic 99 Star ingots; sell (45c) Forgotten Schematic Epic 99 Use it to learn a rare recipe Rift Shard Epic 99 From the vaults behind the rifts: Rift Blade, Rift Tonic, arrows; sell (30c) Rift Blade Epic 1 Melee 24, spirit, reach 1.7; see combat.md Rift Tonic Rare 99 +45 health, +10 thirst, cures poison Every rare material now has a use in a recipe. Sell prices come from SELL_PRICES in src/systems/TradeSystem.ts. Items without a price can't be sold. Inventory and hotbar src/systems/InventorySystem.ts has 24 slots (INVENTORY_SIZE). The first 8 (HOTBAR_SIZE) form the hotbar. add() tops up existing stacks first, then fills empty slots, and returns what didn't fit. remove() takes from the last slots first, and only if the whole amount is there. In the menu, click a slot and then another to move it. Equal stacks merge; anything else swaps. Weapons carry durability, which drops by one per attack. At 0 the weapon breaks and its slot empties. A worn weapon shows a durability bar in its slot. Sort bag (beside the load line) tidies every slot below the hotbar (Inventory.sort()): like items merge into full stacks (worn weapons never stack), then weapons, tools, armour, food, materials and treasure (Epic and above) follow in that order, rarest first, then by name. The hotbar is left alone. Weight Every item has a carry weight (weightOf in src/items/Items.ts): set per item for some (coin 0.01, arrow 0.1, herb 0.1, schematic 0.1, wood 0.5, coal 0.6, stone 0.8, ores and ingots 1, star ingot 1, lantern 1, star metal 1.5, idol 2), set per piece for armour (leather cap and boots 1, tunic and trousers 2; iron boots 3, helmet 4, greaves 6, chestplate 10; starforged boots 2, helm 3, greaves 4, plate 7) and shields (chitin 2, wooden 3, iron 7), otherwise by kind: melee weapons and tools 3, bows 2, food 0.3, everything else 0.5. The player can carry 100, plus 25 per Pack Mule rank. Over that the player is overloaded: 60% speed and no running, with an \"overloaded\" badge on the HUD. The bag shows the load under the grid. Dropping and picking up Q drops the selected hotbar stack in front of the player; the bag's Drop button drops the selected stack. Nothing can be dropped indoors. Dropped items lie on the ground as bobbing icons (src/world/GroundItems.ts). Walking over one picks it up, once the player has first stepped 1.4 tiles away from it, so a drop isn't picked straight back up. A full bag leaves the rest on the ground. Weapons keep their durability on the ground. Up to 300 dropped stacks are kept (the oldest go first), and they are saved with the world. Tools are never equipped: the best one carried applies (toolTier()). Equipment The bag's left panel holds the equipment: head, chest, legs and feet down one side, the off hand on the other, and between them a preview of the player wearing it all, with the armour total underneath (Inventory.equipment). Equip: select an item in the bag and press Equip (or Hold in off hand), or select it and click its slot. Whatever was there goes back to the bag. Click a filled slot to take it off. Armour only protects while worn: every piece adds its share, up to 60% in all (armor()). Equipped items still count toward carry weight. Off hand: a shield (stronger blocks, see combat.md), or a torch or lantern. A whole stack of torches goes into the off hand, and burns down from there. On the player: each worn piece is drawn over the figure in every direction and walk frame: leather in browns, iron in steel with a red crest, starforged in violet plate with gold trim (ARMOR_SETS = 3). The off-hand item is held in the other hand (a shield at the side, a torch up, a lantern hanging), and the shield comes up in front while blocking. Saves keep what is worn. Older saves put on the best armour carried for each slot. Keys 1–8 select a hotbar slot, and its name (and a weapon's durability) shows above the bar for a moment. F, or tapping the selected slot again, uses it. Food is eaten, a Forgotten Schematic is read; tools and other items just show a notice. The selected slot is also the weapon used for attacks. Crafting src/systems/CraftingSystem.ts. The player never builds, so stations are found in the world instead: Campfire: found in camps and nomad camps. Anvil: found in villages, castles and nomad camps. A station counts if its decoration is within 3 tiles of the player (STATION_RANGE). Indoors, only the room's own furniture counts, not the world outside the door. \"By hand\" recipes work anywhere. The menu's Crafting tab lists every known recipe, what you have against what you need, and the station each requires. Crafting removes the inputs, then adds the output. If the output doesn't fit, everything is put back and the result is full. Next to Craft, a ×N button crafts as many as the bag's materials allow, up to 20, when that is more than one. Output Count Station Inputs Stone Axe 1 By hand 3 Wood, 2 Stone Stone Pickaxe 1 By hand 3 Wood, 3 Stone Herbal Bandage 1 By hand 2 Herb Roast Mushroom 1 Campfire 2 Mushroom Forest Stew 1 Campfire 2 Mushroom, 1 Herb, 1 Berries Iron Ingot 1 Anvil 2 Iron Ore, 1 Coal Iron Axe 1 Anvil 2 Iron Ingot, 2 Wood Iron Pickaxe 1 Anvil 3 Iron Ingot, 2 Wood Wooden Sword 1 By hand 5 Wood Hunting Bow 1 By hand 3 Wood, 2 Spider Silk Arrow 6 By hand 1 Wood, 1 Stone Leather Tunic 1 By hand 5 Hide Leather Cap 1 By hand 2 Hide Leather Trousers 1 By hand 3 Hide Leather Boots 1 By hand 2 Hide Wooden Shield 1 By hand 6 Wood Antidote 1 By hand 2 Herb, 1 Berries Cooked Meat 1 Campfire 1 Raw Meat Iron Sword 1 Anvil 3 Iron Ingot, 1 Wood Iron Chestplate 1 Anvil 5 Iron Ingot, 2 Hide Iron Helmet 1 Anvil 2 Iron Ingot Iron Greaves 1 Anvil 3 Iron Ingot, 1 Hide Iron Boots 1 Anvil 2 Iron Ingot Iron Shield 1 Anvil 3 Iron Ingot, 2 Wood Torch 3 By hand 1 Wood, 1 Coal Lantern 1 Anvil 1 Iron Ingot, 2 Spirit Essence Health Potion 1 By hand 2 Herb, 1 Spirit Essence, 1 Berries Frost Draught 1 By hand 2 Ice Shard, 1 Herb Arrow 8 By hand 1 Bone, 1 Wood Chitin Shield 1 By hand 4 Chitin, 2 Wood Star Ingot 1 Anvil 1 Star Metal, 2 Gold Ore, 1 Coal Starforged Axe 1 Anvil 2 Star Ingot, 2 Wood Starforged Pickaxe 1 Anvil 2 Star Ingot, 2 Wood Starforged Helm 1 Anvil 2 Star Ingot Starforged Greaves 1 Anvil 3 Star Ingot, 1 Hide Starforged Boots 1 Anvil 2 Star Ingot Rift Tonic 1 By hand 1 Rift Shard, 2 Herb Arrow 10 By hand 1 Rift Shard, 2 Wood Rift Blade 1 Anvil 1 Iron Sword, 4 Rift Shard, 1 Crystal Star metal comes from meteorites (and some legends), so the starforged tier is for the far lands. Rift shards come only from the vaults of the outer dimensions (dimensions.md). Nothing can be crafted at a station inside a dimension: only by hand. Rare recipes Recipes marked rare in RECIPES stay hidden until learned. The Crafting tab says how many are still to discover. Using a Forgotten Schematic (from the hotbar or the bag) teaches one unknown rare recipe at random and says where to make it; with none left to learn, the schematic is kept. Schematics are in the Epic chest pool and are dropped by every legend and the Arena Guardian. Learned recipes are saved in progression (recipes). Output Count Station Inputs Crystal Bow 1 Anvil 2 Crystal, 3 Spider Silk, 2 Wood Frost Blade 1 Anvil 1 Iron Sword, 4 Ice Shard, 1 Crystal Venom Blade 1 Anvil 1 Iron Sword, 4 Venom Sac Spirit Blade 1 Anvil 1 Iron Sword, 4 Spirit Essence, 1 Crystal Starforged Sword 1 Anvil 3 Star Ingot, 1 Gemstone Starforged Plate 1 Anvil 4 Star Ingot, 2 Chitin Loot src/systems/Loot.ts. Chests A chest makes 2–4 rolls, plus one more per two difficulty bands (floor(band / 2)). Each roll picks a rarity from the spec's chest table, then an item from that rarity's pool. Rarity Chance Common 60% Uncommon 25% Rare 10% Epic 4% Legendary 1% Rarity Pool (count range) Common Wood 2–5, Stone 2–5, Berries 2–4, Herb 1–3, Coal 1–3, Mushroom 1–3, Arrow 3–8 Uncommon Iron Ore 1–3, Herbal Bandage 1–2, Roast Mushroom 1–2, Iron Ingot 1–2, Stone Axe, Wooden Sword, Antidote 1–2, Leather Tunic, Leather Cap, Leather Boots, Wooden Shield Rare Gold Ore 1–2, Health Potion, Forest Stew 1–2, Stone Pickaxe, Hunting Bow, Leather Trousers, Iron Helmet, Iron Boots Epic Forgotten Schematic, Crystal, Gemstone, Iron Pickaxe, Iron Axe, Iron Sword, Iron Chestplate, Iron Greaves, Iron Shield Legendary Golden Idol Chest contents are seeded by the chest's object id and layout index, so a chest always holds the same loot. Chests never refill. Opening one marks its object's anchor in WorldManager.lootedAnchors. Every chest that later regenerates from that anchor starts open and empty (emptyChests). An open chest shows as ChestOpen and answers \"Already looted\". Barrels Breaking a barrel gives nothing 35% of the time. Otherwise it gives one roll: Common (85%) or Uncommon (15%). Buried treasure Digging up an X gives 10–40 coins plus a chest roll at band 2. Treasure comes from the treasure world event (living-world.md). Enemy drops See combat.md. Light At night the player carries a light radius (LivingWorld.playerLight()): Source Radius (tiles) Nothing (night eyes) 2.4 Torch 5 Lantern 7 The largest radius applies. A new world starts with a Lantern in the bag. Torches burn down: for every 2 in-game hours with darkness above 0.5, one torch is used up, unless a Lantern is already lighting the way. See also: README.md · combat.md · living-world.md · objects-and-structures.md "},{"slug":"combat","title":"Combat","group":"Gameplay","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"controls","text":"Controls"},{"level":2,"id":"damage","text":"Damage"},{"level":3,"id":"dealing-damage","text":"Dealing damage"},{"level":3,"id":"taking-damage","text":"Taking damage"},{"level":2,"id":"weapons","text":"Weapons"},{"level":2,"id":"damage-types","text":"Damage types"},{"level":2,"id":"enemies","text":"Enemies"},{"level":2,"id":"wildlife","text":"Wildlife"},{"level":3,"id":"ai-states","text":"AI states"},{"level":3,"id":"difficulty-scaling-and-elites","text":"Difficulty scaling and elites"},{"level":2,"id":"spawning","text":"Spawning"},{"level":2,"id":"arenas","text":"Arenas"},{"level":2,"id":"guarded-places","text":"Guarded places"},{"level":3,"id":"legends","text":"Legends"},{"level":2,"id":"progression","text":"Progression"},{"level":3,"id":"skills","text":"Skills"}],"text":"Combat Attacks, defence, weapons, enemies and their AI, spawning, guarded places, and progression. Code: src/systems/CombatSystem.ts, src/entities/Enemy.ts, src/entities/Species.ts, src/generation/EnemyGenerator.ts, src/game/Player.ts, src/systems/Progression.ts. Controls Action Keyboard and mouse Touch Attack Left click (toward the mouse) or J (the way you face) Hit (aims at the nearest enemy in reach) Block Hold right click or K Hold Block Dodge roll Space Roll Choose weapon 1–8 selects the hotbar slot Tap a slot Attacking with the mouse turns the player toward the cursor. On touch screens, Hit turns the player toward the nearest enemy within the weapon's reach plus 1.5 tiles (animals and burrowed lurkers are skipped), or the way they face if there is none. The weapon is whatever sits in the selected hotbar slot; anything else counts as fists. J and K are the default keys; both can be changed in Settings (README.md). There is no fighting indoors (enemies stay outside) or while swimming (\"You can't fight while swimming\"). Inside an outer dimension the fight moves there: see Arenas and dimensions.md. Damage Dealing damage playerHitDamage(base, levelBonus, crit) = round(base × levelBonus × (crit ? 2 : 1)) Crit chance: 10% per hit (CRIT_CHANCE). A crit doubles the damage. Level bonus: see Progression. Melee arc: a swing hits every enemy within reach + 0.4 tiles and within 60° either side of the swing direction. Enemies closer than 0.3 tiles are always hit. Knockback: each hit pushes the enemy 0.35 tiles along the swing, unless that spot is solid. In a dimension, a walker knocked out over the void falls and dies (a kill, with XP and drops); bosses keep their footing. Burrowed enemies can't be hit. Damage types: see below. The type's factor is applied last, and a hit always does at least 1. Taking damage incomingDamage(raw, armor, blocked, pass) = max(1, round(raw × (1 − armor) × (blocked ? pass : 1))) Armor: the pieces worn add up (see survival.md), to at most 60%. A full leather set gives 20%, a full iron set 40%, a full starforged set 56%. Blocking: without a shield, absorbs 70% of a hit (pass 0.3) and costs 8 stamina. A Wooden Shield in the off hand absorbs 85% for 6 stamina; a Chitin Shield 90% for 5; an Iron Shield 95% for 4. While blocking, the shield is raised in front of the player. It only works if the player faces the attacker and has the stamina. Blocking also halves movement speed and stops running, and a blocked hit inflicts no status effect. After a hit: the player is knocked back 0.45 tiles and can't be hit again for 0.6 s (the sprite blinks). Dodge roll: a 0.18 s dash at 15 tiles/s, during which the player can't be hit. It costs 20 stamina (12% less per Nimble rank) and has a 0.7 s cooldown. Projectiles: enemy projectiles deal 80% of the enemy's damage. Weapons Weapon Kind Damage Type Reach / range Cooldown Durability Fists melee 5 – 1.1 0.45 s – Wooden Sword melee 9 – 1.4 0.45 s 60 Iron Sword melee 20 – 1.6 0.4 s 220 Frost Blade melee 22 frost 1.6 0.4 s 260 Venom Blade melee 22 venom 1.6 0.4 s 260 Spirit Blade melee 22 spirit 1.6 0.4 s 260 Rift Blade melee 24 spirit 1.7 0.4 s 280 Starforged Sword melee 32 – 1.7 0.38 s 420 Hunting Bow bow 12 – 9 0.65 s 140 Crystal Bow bow 20 – 11 0.55 s 260 The three elemental blades, the Starforged Sword and the Crystal Bow are rare recipes, learned from Forgotten Schematics (survival.md). The Rift Blade is forged at an anvil from an Iron Sword, 4 Rift Shards and a Crystal, shards from the vaults behind the rifts (dimensions.md). Durability: each attack with a weapon costs one point. At 0 it breaks and a notice says so. A worn weapon shows a bar along the bottom of its slot (red below 25%). Stats on screen: the bag's item details list a weapon's damage and type, reach or range, hits per second and durability. Selecting a hotbar slot briefly shows the item's name, and a weapon's durability, above the bar. Bows: each shot uses one Arrow from the bag. Without arrows the attack fails with \"No arrows\". Arrows fly at 14 tiles/s and stop at their range or at anything solid. They can crit like melee hits. Damage types A weapon can carry a damage type (Element in src/items/Items.ts): frost, venom or spirit. Species list the types they are weak to (weak, ×1.5 damage) or resist (resist, ×0.5), in src/entities/Species.ts; elementFactor applies them. Arrows from a typed bow would carry its type too, but no bow has one yet. Type Weak to it Resist it Frost Scorpion, Sand Lurker, Jackal, Emperor Scorpion, Dune Tyrant, Thorn Spider Frost Golem, Ancient Golem, Frost Wolf, Tundra Bear Venom Wolf, Bear, Frost Wolf, Tundra Bear, Jackal, Alpha Wolf, Elder Stag Spider, Thorn Spider, Scorpion, Emperor Scorpion, Dune Tyrant, Frost Golem, Ancient Golem, Wraith, Skeleton, Skeleton Archer, Ghoul Spirit Wraith, Skeleton, Skeleton Archer, Ghoul, Glitchling, Shard Wisp, The Rift Warden – The Jackal is weak to both frost and venom. Bandits, wildlife and the Arena Guardian have no weakness or resistance. Enemies Stats below are at band 0. \"Aggro\" is the distance at which the enemy notices the player; \"drops\" are (count, chance). Species Biomes HP Dmg Speed Aggro Behaviour Drops XP Wolf Forest, Snow 30 8 4.4 7 Packs of 2–3 that alert each other; flees below 20% HP Raw Meat 1–2 (100%), Hide 1 (70%) 12 Bear Forest 85 18 3.1 5 Slow, heavy hitter Raw Meat 2–3, Hide 1–2 30 Spider Forest 22 6 3.7 6.5 Ranged web (range 5); hits slow you for 2.5 s Silk 1–2, Venom 1 (15%) 10 Scorpion Desert 35 9 3.4 6 Hits poison you for 4 s Chitin 1, Venom 1 (50%) 14 Sand Lurker Desert 45 14 3.8 3.5 Ambush: hides underground until close Chitin 1–2, Bone 1 (50%) 18 Frost Golem Snow 60 12 2.4 7 Ranged ice (range 6); hits slow you for 2 s Ice Shard 1–2, Crystal 1 (5%) 22 Thorn Spider Forest 48 11 3.9 7 Ranged web (range 5); hits poison you for 4 s Silk 2–3, Venom 1 (60%) 20 Jackal Desert 26 7 4.8 7 Packs of 2–4; flees below 25% HP Raw Meat 1, Hide 1 (50%), Bone 1 (30%) 11 Emperor Scorpion Desert 110 18 3 6 Hits poison you for 6 s Chitin 2–3, Venom 1–2, Gemstone 1 (5%) 38 Frost Wolf Snow 42 10 4.5 7.5 Packs of 2–4; flees below 15% HP Raw Meat 1–2, Hide 1–2 (80%), Ice Shard 1 (30%) 16 Tundra Bear Snow 120 22 3 5 Slow, heavy hitter Raw Meat 2–4, Hide 2–3 40 Wraith All 28 10 3.6 8 Night only, fades at dawn; flies over water and walls Spirit Essence 1 (80%), Coin 1–4 (50%) 18 Bandit Event only 45 11 3.9 10 Groups of 3–4 from the ambush event; human sprite Coin 5–15, Arrow 2–6 (40%), Bandage 1 (30%) 20 Skeleton Guard only 40 10 3.2 9 Dungeon guard Bone 1–2, Arrow 2–5 (40%) 16 Skeleton Archer Guard only 30 8 2.8 10 Dungeon guard; shoots arrows (range 7) Bone 1–2, Arrow 3–6 (70%) 18 Ghoul Guard only 70 15 3.6 9 Dungeon guard from band 2; hits poison you for 3 s Bone 2–3, Spirit Essence 1 (40%) 28 Elder Stag Legend only 420 24 4.6 8 Legendary creature, boss Hide 3–5, Gemstone 1, Crystal 1–2, Forgotten Schematic 1, plus a chest roll 300 Alpha Wolf Legend only 380 22 4.8 9 Legendary creature, boss Hide 3–5, Gemstone 1, Star Metal 1, Forgotten Schematic 1, plus a chest roll 280 Dune Tyrant Legend only 520 26 3.2 8 Legendary creature, boss; hits poison you for 6 s Chitin 4–6, Venom 2–3, Gemstone 1, Star Metal 1–2, Forgotten Schematic 1, plus a chest roll 360 Ancient Golem Legend only 620 28 2.2 10 Legendary creature, boss; throws boulders (range 7) Crystal 2–3, Ice Shard 3–5, Star Metal 2, Forgotten Schematic 1, plus a chest roll 400 Arena Guardian Arena only 900 30 2.6 14 Boss; also throws boulders (range 8) Golden Idol 1, Iron Ingot 3–6, Forgotten Schematic 1–2, plus a chest roll 600 Glitchling Dimension only 26 7 5.2 14 Fast swarmer from the cracks of The Hollow; resists venom Spirit Essence 1 (30%), Crystal 1 (5%) 14 Shard Wisp Dimension only 22 9 3.2 14 Floats over the void (not through walls); shoots shards (range 7); resists frost and venom Crystal 1 (12%), Spirit Essence 1 (30%) 18 The Rift Warden Dimension only 700 26 2.8 18 The Hollow's boss; shoots shards (range 8); phases summon adds and quicken it Crystal 2–3, Gemstone 1, Star Metal 1–2, plus a chest roll 500 Spawn weights within a biome: Wolf 4, Bear 1.5, Spider 3, Thorn Spider 1, Scorpion 4, Sand Lurker 2, Jackal 3, Emperor Scorpion 0.8, Frost Golem 3, Frost Wolf 3, Tundra Bear 1.2, Wraith 3. Variants. The newer species borrow an existing creature's sprites (art on the species), with their own tint and size: the Frost Wolf, Jackal and Alpha Wolf are wolves; the Tundra Bear a bear; the Emperor Scorpion and Dune Tyrant scorpions; the Thorn Spider a spider; the Ancient Golem a golem; the Skeleton Archer and Ghoul skeletons. Bosses and elites get a chest roll; a boss's is 3 bands richer. Wildlife Animals (passive species in src/entities/Species.ts) share the enemy code but never start a fight. Skittish animals bolt when the player comes within their range (and warn their herd); defensive ones ignore the player until hurt, then fight back like an enemy. Animal Biomes HP Dmg Speed Range Temper Drops XP Rabbit Forest 8 0 5 4 Skittish Raw Meat 1 3 Deer Forest 24 0 5.2 6 Skittish, herds of 2–3 Raw Meat 1–2, Hide 1 (80%) 7 Boar Forest 38 7 3.8 — Defensive Raw Meat 2–3, Hide 1 (60%) 10 Sand Lizard Desert 10 0 5.4 3.5 Skittish Raw Meat 1, Chitin 1 (30%) 3 Snow Hare Snow 8 0 5 4 Skittish Raw Meat 1, Hide 1 (30%) 3 Mountain Goat Snow, Desert 30 6 4 — Defensive, 1–3 Raw Meat 1–2, Hide 1–2 (80%) 9 Weights within a biome: Rabbit 4, Deer 3, Boar 2, Sand Lizard 4, Snow Hare 4, Mountain Goat 2. Hunting an animal says \"Hunted a …\"; the Hunter skill adds to its drops. Animals never block resting by a campfire. Drops go straight into the bag. What does not fit is left on the ground where the enemy fell, with a notice. AI states Enemy.update() runs a small state machine. State Behaviour Leaves when burrowed Hidden, can't be hit Player within aggro range → aggro patrol Wanders at 35% speed: picks a direction, or stands still half the time, for 1–3 s Player within aggro range → aggro, and the pack is alerted aggro Faces the player and shows \"!\" After 0.4 s → chase chase Runs at the player. Ranged enemies hold position within 60% of their range. Player farther than 2.5× aggro → patrol. In melee reach, or in ranged range but more than twice melee reach, with the attack ready → attack. attack Flickers and shows a red \"!\" during a 0.3 s wind-up, then hits if the player is within attackRange + 0.4, or else fires a projectile if in range After 0.5 s → chase flee Runs away at 110% speed After 3 s → patrol Taking a hit provokes an enemy (patrol or burrowed → aggro). A species with fleeBelow flees instead once its health drops below that share: wolves 20%, frost wolves 15%, jackals 25%. Every enemy except a flier collides with solid tiles, sliding along walls. Fliers cross everything in the overworld; in a dimension they float over the void but walls stop them (EnemyContext.blocksFlyers). speedScale and cooldownScale on an enemy multiply its species' speed and attack cooldown, so a rift boss's phases quicken that one enemy without touching the shared species. Difficulty scaling and elites Bands come from distance to the origin (src/generation/WorldRules.ts): Band Distance 0 Easy 0–1,000 tiles 1 Medium 1,000–5,000 tiles 2 Hard 5,000–10,000 tiles 3 Extreme 10,000+ tiles Per band: Health: round(hp × (1 + 0.6 × band)) Damage: damage × (1 + 0.5 × band) XP: round(xp × (1 + 0.5 × band)) Elites appear with chance 0.04 + 0.03 × band. Only one member of a spawned group can be elite. An elite has: ×2.5 health and ×1.5 damage, with XP tripled a gold tint and a sprite 1.25× larger doubled drop counts, plus a chest roll at its band Spawning Wild enemies spawn inside the simulation radius; tries happen every 2.5 s. Safe zone: nothing spawns while the player is within 64 tiles of the origin (SAFE_RADIUS), and no spawn point may be within 64 tiles of it. Population cap: round((3 + 2 × band) × (night ? 1.5 : 1)) wild enemies. Guards and event groups don't count toward it. Spawn point: a random open tile in the simulation area, at least 12 tiles from the player (MIN_SPAWN_DISTANCE). The species is a weighted pick from the biome at that tile; wraiths are added at night. Despawn: enemies that leave the simulation area are removed. Night-only enemies are removed at dawn. Wildlife: animals spawn on the same timer, separately: up to 6 by day and 3 at night, anywhere in the simulation area (the safe zone included), at least 12 tiles from the player. They never count toward the enemy cap. Arenas CombatSystem fights on an Arena: what walkers, fliers, knockback and shots collide with, where drops that do not fit go, and whether the overworld's own rules run (wild). The overworld is combat.overworld; a trip into a dimension (RiftTrip, dimensions.md) is another. enterArena stashes the overworld's enemies and drops its shots; while away, no wild spawning, despawning by the simulation area, guard triggers or legend rolls run, and an overworld ambush or invasion that fires meanwhile waits in the stash. leaveArena drops the dimension's enemies and brings the overworld's back exactly as they were. Enemies a dimension asks for are added with add and taken away without a kill with remove; a kill also tells the arena (killed), which reports it to the run. Guarded places Walking into some places wakes their guards (GUARDED_KINDS): Place Guards Dungeon 2–3 Skeletons and 1–2 Skeleton Archers on open floor tiles, plus 1 Ghoul at band 2 or 2 at band 3 Boss Arena 1 Arena Guardian at the centre Killing every guard clears the place: the player gets 60 XP and a \"cleared!\" notice the place's anchor goes into WorldManager.clearedAnchors, which is saved Cleared stays cleared: a cleared anchor never wakes guards again, even after its region regenerates. If the player leaves before clearing it, the guards despawn and the place resets for the next visit. Legends Every chunk in the simulation area gets one roll per region visit, with a chance of LEGEND_CHANCE × (1 + band) (0.05% at band 0, 0.2% at band 3) of a legendary creature at the chunk's centre. Rolls only run when the simulated area moves. Which legend depends on the land (legendFor in src/systems/CombatSystem.ts): Land Legend Desert, band 1 or more Dune Tyrant Snow, band 1 or more Ancient Golem or Alpha Wolf, half each Elsewhere, band 2 or more Alpha Wolf or Elder Stag, half each Anywhere else (near home) Elder Stag Killing a legend records legend@cx,cy in clearedAnchors, so that spot never spawns another. Every boss killed (legends and the Arena Guardian) counts as a trophy, by species, in progression (trophies). The Quests tab lists them under \"Legends of the land\" (found out of 5), with a tick and a count for each one defeated. Event groups (bandit ambush, village defence) are tracked the same way, without persistence. Clearing them reports to quests; clearing a village defence also pays 30 coins. Progression src/systems/Progression.ts: XP to level up: going from level L to L + 1 takes round(40 × L^1.5) XP. Level cap: 50. Per level: each level adds 8 max health and 5% damage, and a skill point. When you level up, current health rises by the same amount as max health. Skills Skill points are spent in the menu's Skills tab. Each skill has 5 ranks (MAX_RANK); points left = level − 1 − ranks learned. Nine skills × 5 ranks is 45 points, so every level up to 46 buys something. Once every skill is at rank 5, the tab says so. Skill Per rank Warrior +8% damage (multiplies the level bonus) Hunter +35% chance of one extra of each drop from animals Gatherer Chopping, mining and picking 15% faster Forager +30% chance of one extra from plants (berries, herbs, mushrooms, cactus fruit) Swimmer Swim 10% faster; deep water tires 15% slower Pack Mule Carry 25 more weight Survivalist Hunger and thirst drain 10% slower Toughness +6% max health (multiplies the level's max health) Nimble Dodge rolls cost 12% less stamina Ranks are saved with progression; older saves load with none. XP source Amount Killing an enemy Species XP, scaled by band and elite Clearing a guarded place or event group 60 Discovering a structure 15 Closing a rift 150 Quests 32–150, see living-world.md See also: README.md · survival.md · living-world.md · objects-and-structures.md "},{"slug":"living-world","title":"Living world","group":"Gameplay","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"time","text":"Time"},{"level":3,"id":"resting","text":"Resting"},{"level":2,"id":"light","text":"Light"},{"level":2,"id":"weather","text":"Weather"},{"level":2,"id":"hazards","text":"Hazards"},{"level":2,"id":"regrowth","text":"Regrowth"},{"level":2,"id":"npcs","text":"NPCs"},{"level":2,"id":"quests","text":"Quests"},{"level":2,"id":"trading","text":"Trading"},{"level":2,"id":"world-events","text":"World events"},{"level":2,"id":"settings","text":"Settings"}],"text":"Living world The clock, light, weather, hazards, regrowth, NPCs, quests, trading, world events and settings. src/game/LivingWorld.ts owns all of these and is driven by the game loop. Opening the menu, a conversation or a vision pauses them. Time src/systems/TimeSystem.ts. One in-game day lasts 960 real seconds (16 minutes), so 1.5 in-game minutes pass per real second. A new world starts on Day 1 at 8:00. Phase Hours Darkness Night 20:00–5:00 1 Dawn 5:00–7:00 Ramps 1 → 0 Day 7:00–18:00 0 Dusk 18:00–20:00 Ramps 0 → 1 The HUD shows the clock and the current weather above the location panel, for example \"Day 3 · 6:40 pm · Rain\". Each in-game hour, LivingWorld.onHour(): advances the weather regrows plants burns torches may start a world event At midnight, each villager has a new quest to offer. Resting Facing a campfire at dusk or night, E rests until 6:00. Resting: costs 15 hunger and 15 thirst, and restores 40 health runs the hourly weather and regrowth for the hours skipped, but no world events is refused while any enemy is hunting the player (wildlife doesn't count) makes this spot the player's waking spot after a collapse (survival.md) plays a 3-second sleep transition (src/ui/SleepOverlay.ts): the screen fades to night over 1 second, \"Z z z\" drifts up for 1 second while the hours pass, then the morning fades back in over 1 second. The game is paused throughout. Beds indoors use the same transition. Light src/rendering/Lighting.ts draws a darkness layer, coloured violet at dusk and deep blue at night, at up to 80% opacity scaled by darkness. Every light cuts a soft hole in it, and warm lights add an orange glow. Thunderstorms add up to 0.45 darkness in daytime. The far lands are grimmer: a faint gloom of 0.03 darkness per difficulty band (0.09 in the Extreme band) is added at all hours, so even midday out there is a little dim. The music darkens with distance too (audio.md). By day (darkness from the clock and storms at 0.01 or less), no lights are gathered: any gloom is drawn as a plain tint. Light source Radius (tiles) Warm glow Player, no light 2.4 none Player with torch / Lantern 5 / 7 yes Campfire 5 strong House window (facade tiles with a window) 1.8 soft Burning tree 6 strong Meteorite 2.5 some With the \"Lighting\" setting off, night is still dark, but as a plain tint (85% as strong) with no lights cut into it. Weather src/systems/WeatherSystem.ts. Each biome type runs its own weather, and the player feels the weather of the biome they stand in. When a spell ends, the next is picked by weight. Clear spells last 4–10 hours; others last 2–6. Biome Weather (weight) Forest Clear 6, Rain 3, Thunderstorm 1, Fog 1.5 Desert Clear 6, Sandstorm 1.5, Heat wave 2 Snow Clear 4, Snow 4, Blizzard 1.2, Fog 1 A heat wave counts as Clear at night. Weather Hunger Thirst Speed Veil Wet Clear ×1 ×1 ×1 0 no Rain ×1 ×0.7 ×1 0.1 yes Thunderstorm ×1.1 ×0.7 ×0.95 0.2 yes Fog ×1 ×1 ×1 0.45 no Sandstorm ×1 ×1.3 ×0.8 0.5 no Heat wave ×1 ×1.6 ×1 0.05 no Snow ×1.15 ×1 ×0.95 0.1 yes Blizzard ×1.35 ×1 ×0.8 0.5 yes Hunger and thirst multiply the drain rates in survival.md. Speed multiplies movement. Veil is how much the screen is hazed. Wet weather slows fire. On screen, src/rendering/ParticleSystem.ts fades weather in and out over a few seconds, with rain, snow or blowing-sand particles. The \"Weather particles\" setting turns them off. Hazards src/systems/HazardSystem.ts. Lightning, in thunderstorms only: Timing: a strike lands every 5–13 s (the first after 8 s). Target: half the time it picks a tree within 8×6 tiles of the player; otherwise a random point within 7×5 tiles. Warning: a glowing circle marks the spot for 1.2 s. Damage: the strike deals 25 to the player if within 1.3 tiles, unless the player is invulnerable, and lights the tree if there is one. Fire burns trees, bushes, palms and dead bushes: Burn time: 7 s, or twice as fast in wet weather. Damage: 6/s to the player within 1.1 tiles. Spread: halfway through, the fire tries to light every neighbour within 2 tiles, with a 35% chance each, or 5% in wet weather. Afterwards: the tree is gone, as if harvested. Regrowth WorldManager.regrow() runs every in-game hour. Gathered plants grow back while their region stays loaded: Plant Regrows after Bush (berries) 1 day (1,440 min) Flowers (herbs) 1 day Mushroom 1.5 days (2,160 min) Garden vegetables 1 day Everything else stays gone until its region fully unloads and regenerates. NPCs src/entities/NPC.ts, src/systems/NpcSystem.ts. Villages: each village in the simulation radius gets four people, all leaving when the village leaves the radius: Three villagers, in one of three outfits. The first is the village's quest giver. Villagers wander within 9 tiles of the village centre at 2.2 tiles/s. A trader who stays within 2 tiles of the well. A resident in every house, who stays at home day and night (Npc.homebody) and is met inside. Lone houses and cabins are lived in half the time (seeded by the object): one resident, at home day and night. Nomad camps (desert) get two people the same way: a traveller who offers quests, and a trader by the fire. Schedule: from 20:00 to 6:00, villagers and traders walk to a door and go indoors, where the player can visit them (see buildings-and-interiors.md). In the morning they step out of their door again. Travelling merchant: appears through a world event, stands still, and leaves after 180 in-game minutes. Lost traveller: the target of a rescue quest. They appear when their quest location enters the simulation radius, then wait until spoken to. After that they follow the player: at a 1.6-tile gap, at 5 tiles/s jumping back to the player if more than 12 tiles behind They're removed if their quest ends, or if the player gets 120 tiles away before finding them. Press E within 1.8 tiles to talk. The NPC you talk to stops and turns to face you (and you turn to them) until the conversation or trade closes, then waits 2 seconds before going on; everyone else carries on as before. Symbol over an NPC Meaning ! Has something new to say, or a quest to offer $ Trader or merchant ? Lost traveller not yet found ✓ A collect or find quest is ready to hand in Marks are worked out four times a second, not every frame. Quests src/systems/QuestSystem.ts. Getting quests: Each quest giver offers one quest per in-game day. The offer is seeded by village, giver and day, so it's the same every time you ask that day. The player can hold 5 active quests. A full journal turns new offers away. Finished quests are dropped from the journal at the next new day. An unfinished quest can be given up with Abandon in the Quests tab (after a confirmation), which frees its slot. Type Weight Objective Coins (base) XP Collect 3 Bring N of a biome resource, handed in to any villager 10 + 2N 30 + 2N Find 1.5 Bring N of a rare find from the giver's band (below), handed in like a collect quest 40 + 25 × rarity × N 60 + 30 × rarity Slay 3 Kill 3–6 of a non-night species from the giver's biome 15 + 6N 40 + 10N Discover 2 Find ruins, a dungeon, a nomad camp, a castle, the standing stones, a tower, a temple, a mine, a cabin or a lake 30 60 Clear 1 Clear any dungeon 70 120 Rescue 1.5 Find a lost traveller 150–300 tiles from the village and walk them within 12 tiles of it 60 150 Defend 1, only from givers who live in a village Accepting it starts an invasion of the village at once; driving it off completes the quest 60 140 Find quests by band (rarity: Uncommon 1, Rare 2, Epic 3): Band Finds (N) 0 Easy Gold Ore 2–3, Spirit Essence 2–3, Venom Sac 2–3 1 Medium Gold Ore 3–4, Crystal 1, Ice Shard 3–4 2 and beyond Crystal 1–2, Gemstone 1, Star Metal 1–2 Collect resources by biome: Biome Resources (N) Forest Wood 10–20, Berries 5–10, Hide 3–5, Silk 3–5, Herb 4–8 Desert Cactus Fruit 4–8, Chitin 3–5, Stone 10–20, Venom 1–3 Snow Ice Shard 2–4, Wood 10–20, Hide 3–5, Coal 4–8 Rewards: Coins are the base × (1 + 0.5 × band) × a random 0.8–1.2. Each quest also rewards the first item of a chest roll at the giver's band. Completion: quests complete as soon as their objective is met and pay out at once, except collect and find quests, which are handed in through a villager's dialog. Rewards that do not fit in the bag are left on the ground. Tracking: the HUD tracker (top right, under the location) lists the story's objective first (story.md), then any treasure or Moonwell waypoint, up to three quests and the tracked map marker. Rescue lines carry an arrow toward the traveller, then toward home. The menu's Quests tab lists everything with its progress and rewards. Trading src/systems/TradeSystem.ts. Talking to a trader or merchant opens the trade panel. Stock: each shop shows 6 offers, seeded by who the trader is. Village traders draw from: Arrows ×10 (12c), Torches ×3 (6c), Bandage (8c), Antidote (12c), Cooked Meat ×2 (10c), Stew (14c), Wooden Sword (20c), Hunting Bow (55c), Leather Armor (60c), Stone Pickaxe (18c). Travelling merchants add rare stock: Health Potion (35c), Iron Sword (140c), Iron Armor (220c), Iron Pickaxe (120c), Lantern (90c), Iron Ingot ×3 (30c). They also carry the first four village items. Buying needs enough coins; if the item doesn't fit in the bag, the purchase is undone. Selling goes one item at a time (Sell), or the whole stack at once (All, which shows the total), and only items with a price are bought: Item Price Golden Idol 250 Star Ingot 120 Gemstone 60 Star Metal 45 Crystal 35 Rift Shard 30 Gold Ore 12 Iron Ingot, Spirit Essence 8 Venom Sac, Ice Shard 6 Iron Ore 4 Hide, Spider Silk, Chitin, Cooked Meat 3 Raw Meat, Bone 1 World events src/systems/WorldEvents.ts. Each in-game hour, once the 3-hour cooldown has passed, there is a 22% chance an event fires. The kind is a weighted pick among those whose conditions hold. \"Wild\" means more than 64 tiles from the origin. Event Weight Condition What happens Meteor 2 Night A meteor streaks in and leaves a 7×7 crater 10–18 tiles away, with a Meteorite to mine Bandits 2 Wild, not in a village 3–4 bandits appear 11 tiles away, already hunting Invasion 3 Standing in a village 2 groups of 3 biome enemies attack from the village edges; clearing them pays 30 coins. A defend quest starts the same invasion. Treasure 2 Always An X is buried 18–32 tiles away, with a waypoint and a compass hint Merchant 1.5 Daytime, no merchant nearby A travelling merchant sets up 6–11 tiles away Ancient 1 Wild A 9×9 Risen Obelisk with a chest rises 12–20 tiles away Moonwell 1.5 Night A Moonwell rises 14–24 tiles away, with a waypoint Rift 0.5 Wild \"A rift tears open nearby.\": a 7×7 rift opens 14–24 tiles away (dimensions.md) Event structures are temporary objects placed on open ground clear of other objects (trees and bushes under them are cleared). They come and go like any other object. Night-only structures. The Moonwell (NIGHT_ONLY in src/systems/WorldEvents.ts) exists only at night: a 9×9 ring of pale stone around a glowing spring, with mushrooms and flowers. Every Moonwell still standing fades at the first hour of daylight. Facing it, E drinks: half of max health back, +40 thirst, poison cured and 25 XP, once per Moonwell (the used ones are saved). Drinking also makes it the waking spot after a collapse. Settings The menu's Settings tab (src/game/Settings.ts) stores these in this browser's localStorage: Setting Default Lighting (off: a plain night tint) on Weather particles (also fireflies and falling leaves) on Hit and dust particles (bursts) on Cap at 30 frames per second off (the cap is otherwise 60) Screen flashes (lightning, getting hurt, a rift's colour slips and HUD stutter) on, or off when the system asks for reduced motion Show the controls bar on Music volume 0.35 Sound effects volume 0.6 Text and HUD size 1 (0.8 to 1.5; applied when the slider is let go) Keys (desktop only) WASD, Shift, J, K, Space, E, F, Q, Tab, M, = and − The size setting zooms the whole HTML interface (#ui, CSS zoom); the touch stick undoes it so it stays where the thumb is. The keys are described in README.md. ?fps=30 in the URL also turns on the frame cap. For testing, ?hour=22 sets the clock and ?weather=storm forces the weather in every biome. See also: README.md · survival.md · combat.md · story.md · rendering.md · saves.md "},{"slug":"buildings-and-interiors","title":"Buildings and interiors","group":"Gameplay","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"building-specs","text":"Building specs"},{"level":2,"id":"drawing","text":"Drawing"},{"level":2,"id":"walls-with-height","text":"Walls with height"},{"level":2,"id":"entering","text":"Entering"},{"level":2,"id":"rooms","text":"Rooms"},{"level":2,"id":"mines","text":"Mines"},{"level":2,"id":"castle-keeps","text":"Castle keeps"},{"level":2,"id":"residents","text":"Residents"},{"level":2,"id":"saving","text":"Saving"}],"text":"Buildings and interiors How houses, cabins, keeps and city buildings are drawn, and how the player walks inside them. Building specs A structure layout can carry buildings (Layout.buildings, BuildingSpec in src/generation/StructureGenerator.ts): Field Meaning x, y, w, h The footprint, in tiles from the layout's top-left door The door's column, counted from the footprint's left edge; the door is in the bottom row style house, cabin, stone, keep, city or mine seed Seeds the building's look and its interior building() fills the footprint with Tile.Building (solid, hidden under the sprite), puts a Path doorstep below the door, and records the spec. Structure Buildings and style Small House One house Cabin One cabin Village Rows of houses along the streets Castle The keep (keep), two barracks (stone) and a stable (cabin) Giant City, Lost City One city building per block, except under the central plaza. In a Lost City about 30% have fallen to rubble. Mine One mine entrance (5 × 3, door in the middle) Buildings always face the camera, so layouts with buildings never rotate (rotated() refuses them); they can still mirror, and mirrored() flips each spec and its door. blit() copies a layout into a bigger one, buildings included. Drawing BuildingRenderer (src/rendering/Buildings.ts) paints each building once into a canvas texture. Everything about its look comes from the building's seed (lookOf()), so a house always looks the same: Style Walls Roofs Extras house Clapboard siding in one of seven colours with white corner boards (50%), red brick with stone quoins (18%), a plastered cottage on a fieldstone footing (18%), or plaster (14%) A-frame; seven roof colours Shutters (60%), flower boxes (60%), a covered porch on posts (45%), wall lamp (50%), moss (20%) cabin Stacked logs with round, ringed ends A-frame Covered porch, shutters, lamp, moss on the roof (60%) city Plaster with an exposed timber frame and corner braces A-frame (50%), hip or side gable; terracotta or slate Shutters, flower boxes, lamp, weathervane stone Fitted stone blocks Slate hip Arched windows, wall lamp keep Fitted stone blocks, two storeys Battlements over a flagstone roof walk; round corner turrets with slate cones and pennants See below The keep (paintKeep()) rises 50 pixels above its footprint and reaches 6 pixels past it on each side. Its two-storey wall has a string course between the floors, a splayed plinth, battlements with their shadow, and a roof walk behind them with a back parapet. In the middle is a great arched double door (voussoirs, studs, a raised portcullis showing its teeth) on a wide step, flanked by long red banners with a gold emblem. Windows are tall and arched on the ground floor and smaller above, all lit at night. Ivy climbs the old stone. At each end stands a round turret, shaded like a cylinder, with arrow slits, a ring of corbels, a slate cone (snow-capped in the snow) and a red pennant. Roofs. All roofs are staggered courses of chunky clay tiles (makeShingles(), 7 × 6 pixels): each tile has a lit top lip, a seam and highlight at its side, rounded lower corners, and a shadow where the next course overlaps it, and each varies a little in tone. Roofs are lighter at the top and darker toward the eaves; slopes facing the light (the front, the left hip, the left side of a gable) are bright and the others much darker. The roof's thickness shows along every eave (eaveEdge(): tile ends over a dark underside) and above every bargeboard, the ridge is a rounded cap of short tiles, and the eaves cast a deep shadow onto the walls below. Chimneys show a front face, a darker side face and a capped top. An A-frame (aFrame(), every house and cabin) is a peaked roof with its gable facing the viewer: below the white bargeboards is the gable wall, a triangle with a round attic window; above them the roof surface slopes back to a rounded ridge, so the building's outline is peaked, not a box. Its pitch (40–52% of the roof's height is gable, the rest roof surface) varies by seed, its tile courses run parallel to the slopes, and the left slope is lit while the right is in shade. A chimney rises from one slope. A covered porch is a small A-frame over the door on two wooden posts, open and shaded underneath. A hip roof (barracks, some city buildings) slopes at both ends; a side gable runs straight across between bargeboards; both may get a dormer and a chimney. Snow. Buildings standing in a snow biome are painted with snow on the roof (thick near the ridge, a ragged edge), on the chimney cap and dormer, and a few icicles along the eave. Wall height: at least the style's base (30–34 pixels), growing to 46% of the footprint's depth (up to 52), so deep buildings never turn into all roof. Windows sit halfway up the wall, every other column out from the door, with framed glass, a glint, curtains, a sill, and (by look) shutters in the house's accent colour and flower boxes. Door: an arched door in a frame, painted in the accent colour (plain wood on cabins and stone), with planks, iron hinges, a brass knob and a little window, on a stone step; sometimes under a small porch roof, with a wall lamp beside it. Light and shade: shade under the eaves, a darker far end of the wall, a plinth of fitted stones, a dark outline, and a soft shadow cast on the ground to the right. Performance. Painting a house takes about 3 ms. At most two new buildings are painted per frame (the rest appear on the next frames), and up to 48 paintings are cached; older ones that are off screen are freed. Each building is one sprite, so a village costs a handful of draw calls. A mine is drawn differently: a rock face with a ragged, mossy top and boulders, a tunnel mouth that darkens inward with rails running into it, a timber frame (posts, lintel and corner braces), a sign with a pick and hammer, a hanging lantern (its night light) and rubble at the foot. The sprite overhangs the footprint by 4 pixels on each side (plus 6 for the cast shadow) and rises 18 pixels above it for the roof and chimney. Sprites are created for buildings that touch the render area, sorted by their footprint's bottom edge so the player walks in front of and behind them, and drawn at 50% opacity while the player is behind one. At night every window, attic window, dormer and wall lamp is a warm light (radius 1.8 tiles). Walls with height Outside of buildings, stone and wood walls (castle walls, towers, ruins, city walls) are tiles. TileRenderer draws a wall with a matching wall below it as its top (capstones, or log ends) and a wall with anything else below it as its front face (StoneWallFace, WoodWallFace: bricks or planks, a lit lip and a shadow at the foot). Walls two tiles thick therefore read as raised walls. A chunk's bottom row looks at the chunk below, and the chunk above is redrawn when its lower neighbour appears. Entering Facing a building's door outdoors shows E Enter. Pressing E builds an Interior (src/world/Interior.ts) and switches the view: The world view hides and the interior view shows; the player sprite moves into the room. The world keeps streaming around the door outside, so nothing unloads. The clock, events, NPC schedules and vitals keep running (LivingWorld.updateIndoors). Weather does not reach indoors, and enemies outside wait. Lighting is dimmer than outdoors at night (60% of the night's darkness), lit by the room's fireplace and lamps. There is no fighting indoors: attacks do nothing, since combat still runs at the door outside. Crafting stations count only if the room itself has them, not the world outside the door. What does not fit in the bag (loot, rewards) is left on the ground outside the door. Walking out over the doormat leaves, onto the doorstep outside. Saving indoors records the spot outside the door, so a reload starts outside. Interior is a Place (src/game/Place.ts), like the overworld and a trip into an outer dimension: the game asks the current place for the player's collision, its arena (none indoors) and its spot in the overworld (the doorstep), which saving, streaming, the HUD, the clock and what does not fit in the bag all go by. An outer dimension behind a rift is entered much like a room, but with fighting, hazards and a map; see dimensions.md. Rooms Interiors are generated from the building's seed, so a room is the same every visit: Part Rule Size Building size plus 4, between 9 × 8 and 16 × 12 tiles Walls A dark ceiling edge around the room, and two rows of wallpaper over wainscot at the back Floor Wood for houses and cabins; stone for stone buildings, keeps and city buildings Rug A 4 × 3 rug in the middle, and a doormat at the exit Back wall A fireplace, a bed, and one or two bookshelves (three in a keep, plus a banner) Middle A table with a chair on each side Corners Potted plants, crates and barrels; sometimes a lamp Chest Chance by style: house 50%, cabin 60%, stone 40%, keep 80%, city 50% The walk from the door is always kept clear. Thing E does Bed Sleep until morning, at dusk or night; otherwise \"too early\". The spot outside the door becomes the waking spot after a collapse. Bookshelf Read a line from one of the books (BOOKS in src/world/Interior.ts) Chest Loot it once. Interior chests use ids from 1,000,000 up in the object's opened set, and follow the never-refill rule with the rest. Resident Talk to them Mines A mine entrance leads into a cave instead of a room (Interior.dig): Part Rule Size 20 × 16 tiles Shape A main chamber (an ellipse with ragged edges) and four random side pockets, plus a three-wide tunnel from the entrance Rock CaveWall (a rock face) where rock has floor below it, RockDark beside the floor, CaveRoof (near black) deeper in Floor MineFloor (dirt and gravel), 8% Rubble; Rail up the tunnel to a mine cart Props A timber SupportBeam at every fourth spot along the back walls Against the walls 3 lamps, 6–9 ore, 1–3 crystals (55% of mines), 3–5 stones, a crate, a barrel, and a chest 55% of the time; never next to each other Anything the player could not walk up to from the entrance is removed. Caves are always dark (at least 75% darkness, more at night), lit by the lamps, the player's light, and daylight from the entrance. Ore, crystals and stones are mined as outdoors (same tools, times and drops; see survival.md), with a progress bar over the rock. GatheringSystem.ground is the Interior while inside, so drops are seeded per mine and tile. Rarer finds depend on the difficulty band of the mine's door outside (GatheringSystem.bandAt). Mined rock is recorded in the object's opened set (ids from 2,000,000 up) and stays gone for as long as the mine stands. Castle keeps A keep's door leads into a building of three floors, 22 × 16 tiles each, joined by stairs (Interior.keepFloor()). Facing stairs, E goes up or down (E Go up to the upper floor); the HUD shows the floor's name, and the player arrives by the stairs on the other floor. Only the great hall has the way out. Floor Name What is there 1 Upper Floor Wooden floors and papered walls; a corridor with a runner, lamps and a banner, off which open a library (four bookshelves, a reading table), a bedchamber (two beds, a fireplace, a rug, a chest) and an armory (weapon racks, an armour stand, a training dummy, and the stairs down) 0 Great Hall A red carpet from the door to a gilded throne on a dais between banners; two rows of pillars; feast tables with chairs on both sides; fireplaces; armour stands; lamps; stairs up and down −1 Cellar Dark (like a mine); rows of barrels and crates, pillars, scattered rubble, lamps, and two treasure chests Floors are generated from the building's seed and the floor number, so each is the same every visit. Chests and mined rock on each floor have their own ids in the object's opened set (floor 0 keeps the old ids, so saves still work). Anything that cannot be walked up to from the stairs or door is removed. Barracks have a row of bunks along the back wall, a weapon rack and an armour stand, a table with chairs, a barrel, a crate, a lamp, and sometimes a chest. Residents Every village house has someone at home day and night, and half of lone houses and cabins are lived in (see living-world.md). Villagers who went home for the night join them inside the building whose door they went through. They stand at spots around the table and can be talked to as usual: quests, hand-ins and trade all work indoors. Saving Buildings are not saved: like every structure, they regenerate from the seed. Looted chests inside stay looted through the object's anchor (saves.md). See also: README.md · objects-and-structures.md · rendering.md · living-world.md "},{"slug":"exploration","title":"Exploration","group":"Gameplay","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"fog-of-war","text":"Fog of war"},{"level":2,"id":"the-map","text":"The map"},{"level":2,"id":"markers","text":"Markers"},{"level":2,"id":"compass","text":"Compass"},{"level":2,"id":"saving","text":"Saving"}],"text":"Exploration The map with fog of war, map markers, the compass, and rewards for exploring (spec 6.4). Fog of war ExplorationSystem (src/systems/ExplorationSystem.ts) remembers every chunk the player has seen. Each frame it walks the render area (ChunkManager.renderRect) and records any chunk not yet seen, keyed by its chunk key, with the biome palette it had at that moment (paletteOf(type, variant) + 1). Seen chunks stay on the map after the world unloads them. Biome types are fixed per area (see world-generation.md), so the recorded colours stay accurate even when the area regenerates. Every EXPLORE_STEP (50) newly seen chunks grants EXPLORE_XP (20) experience and a notification. The map Maps only work inside the outer dimensions behind the rifts. In the overworld the Map tab is hidden and M only says \"Maps don't work here: the world won't hold still.\" (Game.mapAvailable is true only on a trip into a dimension). Fog of war, exploration XP and markers are still recorded for the overworld. Inside a dimension the Map tab and M show that space instead (src/ui/DimensionMap.ts): the tiles the player has seen, one colour per kind of tile, framed on what has been seen so far; each chamber seen into by name, ticked once done; the rift home (pink), the portal (pale cyan once open) and the player (white). Fog is a byte per tile (RiftTrip.seen): when the player reaches a new tile, every tile within 6 is marked seen, and a version number rises only if something new was seen. The seen tiles are painted one pixel each into a small canvas only when that version changed, and the map is drawn when the tab opens, never per frame (the menu pauses the game). See dimensions.md. The overworld map (src/ui/MapView.ts), kept for if maps ever work there, draws on a canvas: Layer Drawn as Unexplored Dark background Explored chunks A square per chunk in its biome colour Markers Red pins; the tracked one is brighter The player A white dot Drag to pan. Zoom with the + and − buttons, the mouse wheel, or a two-finger pinch on the map (2 to 40 pixels per chunk, opening at 16). The tile coordinates under the cursor show below the map. \"Centre on me\" recentres on the player. Pointer positions are measured against the canvas's shown size, so taps and markers land in the right place when a small screen shows the map scaled down. Redraws are batched: however many pointer or wheel events arrive, the map draws at most once per frame (requestDraw). Markers Clicking or tapping the map without dragging drops a marker there, named \"Marker N\". The list beside the map tracks or removes each one. A tracked marker gets a line with a direction arrow in the quest tracker. Compass The compass sits at the top left of the HUD. North is always up (the view never rotates). The needle points toward the tracked marker, if there is one, or else toward the story's objective: the nearest open rift loaded (story.md). Next to it are the player's tile coordinates and the marker's name. Saving Explored chunks (as flat key/value pairs), markers and the tracked marker are saved with the game; see saves.md. See also: README.md · story.md · living-world.md · rendering.md "},{"slug":"audio","title":"Audio","group":"Gameplay","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"setup","text":"Setup"},{"level":2,"id":"sound-effects","text":"Sound effects"},{"level":2,"id":"music","text":"Music"},{"level":2,"id":"ambience","text":"Ambience"}],"text":"Audio Synthesised sound effects, generated music and weather ambience, all made with the Web Audio API; no audio files ship. Setup AudioSystem (src/audio/AudioSystem.ts) creates its AudioContext on the first key press or pointer press, because browsers block sound until the player interacts. Everything routes through two buses, music and effects, whose volumes come from the Settings tab (defaults: music 0.35, effects 0.6). A shared two-second white-noise buffer feeds the noise-based sounds. Sound effects Sound When Made of step Every 0.36 s walking, 0.25 s running Short low-passed noise chop Each axe strike while gathering (every 0.45 s) A falling triangle thump and band-passed noise mine Each pickaxe strike while gathering A metallic triangle ping and high-passed noise pickup Something gathered Two rising square blips swing A melee swing Band-passed noise sweeping down bow A bow shot A falling sawtooth twang hit Damage dealt to an enemy A low sine thud and noise hurt The player takes damage A falling square tone kill An enemy defeated Three falling blips dodge A dodge roll Band-passed noise sweeping up level Level up A rising four-note arpeggio quest Quest complete A three-note chime chest Chest or treasure opened Two triangle notes ui Any button, slot or tab clicked A tiny click thunder A lightning strike Long low-passed noise warn A vital gets worse (low or critical), except stamina; a glyph puzzle resets A two-note square alarm, played twice rift Stepping into or out of a rift A swell of band-passed noise under a falling four-note shimmer Music Music is generated one beat at a time, scheduled slightly ahead so timing stays steady. A beat is 0.75 s, 0.62 s in the desert (brisker) and 0.9 s in the snow (slower). Scale follows the mood: major pentatonic by day, minor pentatonic at night, a Phrygian-flavoured scale in the desert, a Lydian-flavoured one in the snow. Root moves between four roots every 16 beats, an octave lower at night. Further out it drops 6% per difficulty band (up to band 3), so the far lands sound lower and darker. Pad: a soft three-note chord at the start of every bar. Melody: a plucked note on about half the beats (fewer at night). The voice follows the land: a triangle in the forest, a soft square in the desert, a long sine in the snow. Drone: from band 2 out, a low sine two octaves under the root every 8 beats. Danger: a low pulse every other beat while enemies are chasing or attacking the player. Outer dimensions (MusicMood.dimension, from the dimension's mood.music): their own music (riftBeat). Beats are slower (1.05 s); a low detuned drone every 8 beats, and sparse long sine notes from a scale of close, unresolved steps (0, 1, 3, 6, 7, 10 semitones). The danger pulse plays as in the overworld. Ambience A looping filtered noise source plays the weather: low-passed rain for rain and storms (louder in storms), and band-passed wind for sandstorms and blizzards. Its volume follows how strongly the weather is felt, and fades when the game is paused. The volume is only rescheduled when it changes, and the noise stops once it has faded to silence. The land has its own sounds, rolled twice a second (AudioSystem.ambience): Where Sound Forest, by day Birds: two or three quick rising chirps (12% of rolls) Anywhere but snow, at night Crickets: three short high clicks (25% of rolls) Desert A gust of band-passed wind now and then Snow A lower gust of wind now and then Mines Water dripping, and now and then a low drone Near an open rift Its hum: two close low tones beating against each other, with a crackle beside it; louder and more often the nearer the rift (MusicMood.rift, 0 to 1 within 12 tiles, 0 elsewhere) Inside an outer dimension Glassy chimes now and then, nothing else Ambient sounds are skipped while 26 or more voices sound (MAX_VOICES less 6), so they never crowd out the sound effects. Birds, crickets and gusts are quiet indoors and in strong rain or storms. Creature calls. When something hunts the player outdoors, its call sounds, at most every 18 seconds: wolves (and wolf-shaped creatures) howl, wraiths and skeletons wail, bears and golems growl (Game.hunterCall). See also: README.md · living-world.md · rendering.md "},{"slug":"story","title":"Story","group":"Story","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"in-play","text":"In play"},{"level":2,"id":"implementation","text":"Implementation"}],"text":"Story The world has started to forget itself. Paths lead somewhere else from one day to the next, wells move in the night, valleys are not where the old maps put them. It began when rifts tore open all over the land: tears hanging in the air, burning violet at the edges, each one onto an outer dimension. Around them the land stutters and glitches. Something on the other side is calling. Why any of it is happening is left for later: the story hints and explains nothing. For now the player's thread is simple: find a rift, step through, survive what waits inside, and close it. The world is still open to explore around it. In play Objective (HUD tracker) Shown when Arrow Find a rift No rift closed yet, and none within 14 tiles Toward the nearest open rift loaded, if any Enter the rift No rift closed yet, and an open rift within 14 tiles Toward it Survive The Hollow: k/4 chambers Inside a dimension, its way home still shut None Close the rift: step through the portal Inside, once its boss has fallen None Rifts closed: N Once a rift has been closed, in the overworld Toward the nearest open rift loaded, if any The objective is always the tracker's first line, and the compass needle points where its arrow does unless a map marker is tracked (exploration.md). \"Loaded\" means a live rift object, within the cache area around the player; rifts further out are not known. A new world opens with a vision of three pages, Rifts, once the opening screen has cleared (RiftStory.intro). Closing the first rift shows a one-page vision, The Tear Seals: the land holds still for a moment, and the call comes again from further off. Every rift closed counts toward \"Rifts closed\", and is worth 150 XP. Leaving without closing (stepping back onto the rift, or dying inside) leaves the rift open; the objective goes back to \"Enter the rift\". Where rifts come from, how often, and what closing one does to the world: objects-and-structures.md. What is behind them: dimensions.md. Implementation RiftStory (src/story/RiftStory.ts) holds the story's state: rifts closed and whether the player has ever entered one. It is built in LivingWorld's constructor (the seam where a story plugs in) with a function that shows a Vision. Seam What the story does LivingWorld.tracker() objective(at, rift, run) puts the objective first. at is the player's spot in the overworld, rift the centre of WorldManager.nearestRift (live, open rifts only), and run the trip in progress (LivingWorld.trip, set by Game; chambers done, chambers in all, whether the portal is open) Game.loaded() On a new world (not a loaded save, not the playground), intro() after the opening screen Game.enterRift / exitRift onEnter(); onClosed() when the portal closed the rift LivingWorld.save() / load() rift: { version: 1, closed, entered } in the living save; a save without it (from before the rifts) starts at \"Find a rift\", without the intro Nothing in the story runs per frame: the tracker is refreshed with the HUD, four times a second. Testing. The story playground (?playground, development builds; README.md) has the rift tools: \"Rift nearby\" tears one open beside the player, \"Enter the Hollow\" jumps straight into a dimension (through the nearest rift, opening one if need be), and \"→ Combat\", \"→ Path\", \"→ Puzzle\", \"→ Boss\" and \"→ Vault\" skip to that chamber with the ones before it done (DimensionRun.skipTo). The playground shows no intro vision and never saves. See also: README.md · dimensions.md · living-world.md · exploration.md "},{"slug":"dimensions","title":"Dimensions","group":"Story","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"entering-and-leaving","text":"Entering and leaving"},{"level":2,"id":"the-hollow","text":"The Hollow"},{"level":2,"id":"challenges","text":"Challenges"},{"level":2,"id":"rewards","text":"Rewards"},{"level":2,"id":"dying","text":"Dying"},{"level":2,"id":"the-map","text":"The map"},{"level":2,"id":"saving","text":"Saving"},{"level":2,"id":"under-the-hood","text":"Under the hood"}],"text":"Dimensions The outer dimensions behind the rifts: finite spaces of challenges, entered from a rift in the overworld and left through a portal that closes the rift behind the player. Only one exists so far, The Hollow, but dimensions come from a registry so more can be added. Code: src/dimension/ (the dimensions themselves, with no rendering), src/game/RiftTrip.ts (one trip into one), src/rendering/DimensionRenderer.ts, src/ui/DimensionMap.ts, and the dimension parts of src/game/Game.ts. Entering and leaving Facing a rift's tear in the overworld shows E Enter the rift (objects-and-structures.md). Pressing E: Seeds the space from the rift: hash(worldSeed, hashString(rift.id)), so the same rift always leads to the same layout, and pickDimension(seed) chooses which dimension (always The Hollow for now). Makes a RiftTrip and moves the fight there (CombatSystem.enterArena): the overworld's enemies and shots are stashed, untouched, until the player is back. Hides the overworld's sprites and opens the DimensionRenderer, then puts the player on the arrival platform, below the rift they came in by. There are three ways out, all back to the doorstep below the rift in the overworld: Way out The rift The run Step through the portal in the vault (it wakes once the boss falls) Closed: it shows a scar while its region stands, and +150 XP Done Step back onto the arrival rift Still open Lost: entering again starts over Die Still open Lost, and so is everything carried (below) Leaving restores the overworld exactly: the trip's enemies go, the overworld's come back as they were, the overworld renders again (chunks re-culled, buildings rescanned from their cached art, ground items redrawn), and the dimension's meshes and sprites go back to their pools. While inside, the overworld goes on as it does for a player indoors (buildings-and-interiors.md): the clock, hunger, thirst and villagers' days run (LivingWorld.updateIndoors), the world keeps streaming around the rift outside, and world events can still fire there (an ambush waits in the stash). Nothing of the overworld's combat runs: no wild spawning, despawning by distance, guard triggers or legend rolls. There is no weather; hazards, ground items and gathering are the overworld's and stay there. Only by-hand recipes can be crafted, nothing can be dropped, and what does not fit in the bag is left outside the rift. The Hollow A chain of stone chambers floating in a violet void, 96 × 96 tiles (src/dimension/Hollow.ts). Six chambers are laid end to end by a backtracking search from a random start, each joined to the last by a short walled corridor with a three-tile door into the next: Chamber Size (tiles, walls included) Challenge Arrival 11 × 11 None: the rift home, rune tiles and four crystals Glitch nest 17–19 × 15–17 Waves Crumbling path 21–23 square Hazard path Glyph hall 15–17 × 13–15 Glyph puzzle Warden's arena 21–23 × 19–21 Boss Vault 11 × 9 None: the portal home and the vault's chest The HUD's location names the chamber the player stands in (RiftTrip.chamberAt). Light comes from crystals, the rift, safe pads and the portal; the space's darkness is 0.55 at every hour, tinted violet. Its music and sounds are in audio.md. Doors. The door into a chamber stays sealed until the chamber before it is done. The waves and the boss also seal the door behind the player while they fight, and open it again when they are won (or reset). Challenges src/dimension/Challenges.ts. Each challenge chamber has a state machine (idle, active, done); DimensionRun sequences them. Per frame the game calls run.update(dt, px, py) with the player's feet and acts on the events it reports (takeEvents); nothing in a run allocates per frame. Waves (Glitch nest). Walking two tiles into the chamber seals it: \"The doors seal. Something crawls out of the cracks…\". Three waves come out of spawn spots around the walls, away from the player: 4 Glitchlings; 3 Glitchlings and 2 Shard Wisps; 3 Glitchlings and 3 Shard Wisps. The next wave arrives 1.5 s after the last one falls; after the third the doors open. Hazard path (Crumbling path). The room is void, with a ledge at each door and a winding path of floating slabs between them, with a 2 × 2 safe pad at each bend: Crumbling slabs crack when stepped on, fall 0.55 s later, and come back 5 s after that. Glitching slabs blink on a cycle: there for 2 s (flickering for the last 0.5), gone for 1.3 s. Each run of them has a safe step before it to wait on. Walking onto the void, a fallen slab or a glitched-out one is a fall: 15 damage, and the player is put back on the last safe pad touched (a fall also puts every slab back, so the way is open again). Reaching the far ledge finishes it: \"Solid ground again.\" Glyph puzzle (Glyph hall). Four or five of six glyphs are carved on mural stones along a wall without a door; the same glyphs are on pressure plates scattered over the floor. The stones light up one after another in the order to press the plates (0.7 s each, then a pause of two beats), and stay lit as each is done. A wrong plate springs them all back up (\"Wrong order\"). The last right one opens the door. Boss (Warden's arena). Walking three tiles into the arena seals it and wakes The Rift Warden in the centre of its rune circle. Its health drives two phases: Below What happens 66% \"The Warden tears the air open.\" 3 Glitchlings join it 33% \"The Warden flickers with fury.\" 2 Shard Wisps join it, and it attacks faster (cooldown ×0.55) and moves faster (×1.35) At most 6 summons live at once. The phases' multipliers go on that one enemy (Enemy.speedScale, cooldownScale), never on its species. When it falls, its summons fade, the doors open and the portal wakes: \"The way home opens.\" The creatures (stats in combat.md) are as tough as the land around the rift: their difficulty band is the rift's, so rifts far from home are harder. All three are weak to spirit damage. Glitchlings swarm fast; Shard Wisps float over the void (walls still stop them) and shoot shards. A walker knocked off an edge falls and dies; the Warden keeps its footing. Rewards The vault's chest opens with E once the boss has fallen (it opens by itself if the player steps through the portal first). It is rolled once from the run's seed (HOLLOW_REWARDS): Item Count Chance Rift Shard 3–5 Always Coin 20–45 Always Crystal 1–2 60% Gemstone 1 30% Star Metal 1 25% Rift Shards are found nowhere else. They forge a Rift Blade (an Iron Sword, 4 shards and a Crystal at an anvil: 24 spirit damage, reach 1.7), make a Rift Tonic by hand (1 shard, 2 Herbs: +45 health, +10 thirst, cures poison) or 10 arrows (1 shard, 2 Wood), and sell for 30 coins (survival.md). Closing the rift is worth 150 XP, and the Warden counts as a trophy. Dying Dying inside an outer dimension, from a blow or a fall, loses every item in the bag and the hotbar (Inventory.clearCarried). Worn equipment (head, chest, legs, feet and the off hand) is kept. The rift throws the player back out onto its doorstep in the overworld, revived as after any collapse, with an alert: \"The rift took everything you carried.\" The rift stays open; the run is gone. Dying in the overworld is unchanged: the player wakes at their waking spot and keeps everything. The map Maps only work inside an outer dimension: there the Map tab shows and M opens it (exploration.md). DimensionMap (src/ui/DimensionMap.ts) shows: the tiles the player has seen (within 6 tiles of where they have stood), coloured by kind; the void stays dark, each chamber the player has seen into, by name, with a tick once done, the rift home (pink), the portal (pale cyan once open, dark before), and the player (white). It frames what has been seen so far, at a whole number of pixels per tile. Fog is one byte per tile (RiftTrip.seen), updated only when the player reaches a new tile, with a version number that rises only when something new is seen. The seen tiles are painted one pixel each into a small canvas only when that version changed, and the map is drawn when the tab opens (the menu pauses the game), never per frame. Saving Saving inside records the doorstep outside the rift as the player's position, as indoors: a reload starts outside the rift, and the run's progress is gone. Nothing of a trip is saved. See saves.md. Under the hood The registry. DIMENSIONS in src/dimension/Dimension.ts lists every dimension (DimensionDef): its id and name, its mood (darkness, tint, music, palette), size, generator, the creatures met inside, waves, boss and phases, hazard tuning and rewards. pickDimension(seed) chooses one for a rift. A new dimension is a new DimensionDef with its own generator; the challenges, the run, the renderer and the map work on any layout. The ground. A trip is a Place (src/game/Place.ts) and a combat Arena, so the game asks it, not \"are we in a dimension?\", for: Asked for The trip answers The player's collision (isSolidAt) Walls, props and sealed doors; the void is open (walking onto it is a fall) Walking enemies (blocksEnemies) The same, plus the void and every gap in the path Fliers (blocksFlyers) Walls, props and sealed doors Knockback and shots (isSolid, blocksShots) Walls, props and sealed doors; shots fly over the void Falls (isHole) The void, fallen slabs and glitched-out ones The player's spot in the overworld (spot) The rift's doorstep: saving, streaming, the HUD, the clock and overflow go by it Events. RiftTrip.update steps the run, reveals the map around the player, reports the boss's health share, and handles its own events: spawn (a new Enemy at the rift's band, provoked, added to combat and mapped id ↔ enemy), despawn (removed without a kill), bossPhase (the boss's multipliers). Every event then goes to Game.tripEvent: fell hurts the player and moves them to the safe spot, door and plate play sounds, notice and wave show notes, reward puts the items in the bag (overflow outside the rift), closed and leave take the player home. Kills come back the other way: CombatSystem tells its arena (Arena.killed), and the trip tells the run (enemyKilled). Rendering. See rendering.md. Nothing of a dimension runs or is drawn in the overworld, and nothing of the overworld's combat, glitches or decorations runs or is drawn in a dimension. See also: story.md · combat.md · exploration.md · saves.md "}];
